{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C9S7TvubaBX52ijGegZyEv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~October 2026)\n5\t\n6\t## Paper Type\n7\tEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n10\t\n11\t**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: 38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability persists within every subject domain (within-subject C%=31-45%, avg 38.1%), is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02). This means cross-temporal MMLU score comparisons carry uncontrolled item-level variance.\n12\t- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%, enrichment=0.96, sanity=0.18%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%)\n13\t- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 means \"item instability\" may mainly reflect \"models got better at those domains\" — we cannot decompose 38% into contamination vs capability improvement\n14\t\n15\t**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n16\t- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n17\t- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\"\n18\t\n19\t**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n20\t- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n21\t- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n22\t- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n23\t\n24\t**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n25\t1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n26\t2. **DIF items = discriminative signal carriers**: DIF removal causes significantly MORE ranking disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions). DIF items carry 1.09× cross-cohort accuracy swing vs 0.96× for stable items — they concentrate the information that differentiates model generations.\n27\t3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587.\n28\t4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF captures capability, not contamination**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005; partial r=−0.385 controlling accuracy; permutation p=0.014). Contaminated models (high GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma families) perform WORSE on DIF items. DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items that contamination cannot fake — contamination produces shallow performance gains concentrated on non-DIF items. N=33 matched models, leave-one-out robust (min |r|=0.374).\n29\t- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 33 models)\n30\t- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\" — mutual value positioning\n31\t- Caveats (→ limitations): super-family analysis limited to N≥20 families; GSM1K validation N=33 (CI wide); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth\n32\t\n33\t## Story Spine\n34\t\n35\t**Problem**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Item-level behavioral drift across model generations could silently erode this assumption.\n36\t\n37\t**Approach**: We apply Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis — a classical psychometric tool for detecting item-level behavioral anomalies — to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n38\t\n39\t**What we find**: Three discoveries, each a contribution:\n40\t\n41\t1. **38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C DIF, temporal cohorts). This persists within every subject domain (31-45% per subject), is not statistical noise (random split: 0.18%), and cannot be eliminated by three structural corrections (matched-ability, IRT θ-conditioning, bifactor pre-check). The instability correlates with capability improvement (ρ=0.307) — models got better at those domains, changing how items function.\n42\t\n43\t2. **The field's standard validation approach has a blind spot**: Web-overlap contamination labels (Li et al.) measure whether an item is publicly accessible — a global, model-agnostic property. DIF detects whether an item functions differently across model cohorts — a dynamic, group-specific signal. Enrichment≈1.0 between DIF flags and web-overlap labels is expected when most items have equal global exposure (MMLU on Common Crawl since 2020). Semi-synthetic injection proves DIF works (ΔTPR=+0.508) — the problem is the ground truth, not the detector. Any contamination detection paper validating against web-overlap labels at the item level has the same issue.\n44\t\n45\t3. **A semi-synthetic validation framework** that injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices, measuring ΔTPR as the detection power metric. Cross-subject matching eliminates the matching variable contamination problem (ΔFPR=0.000). This infrastructure is reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n46\t\n47\t4. **DIF is a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter**. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after 31% removal), but DIF items concentrate the discriminative signal that differentiates model generations (removal disrupts rankings MORE than random, z=-8 to -49σ). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K confirms: DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination (r=−0.478, p=0.005) — contaminated models can't fake performance on DIF items, which capture genuine capability differences. DIF identifies which capability dimensions are changing — complementing Growing Pains' anchor-item approach by detecting which anchors are degrading.\n48\t\n49\t**Headline result**: \"Psychometric analysis of METABENCH reveals that 38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations. Web-overlap contamination labels do not capture this differential signal (enrichment≈1.0), while semi-synthetic injection confirms detection capability (ΔTPR=+0.508 on MMLU, +0.818 on GSM8K). Cross-benchmark validation using GSM1K contamination gaps shows DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination (r=−0.478, p=0.005): DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items that contamination cannot replicate. We provide a reusable validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000).\"\n50\t\n51\t## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n52\t\n53\tSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n54\t\n55\t## Venue Fit\n56\t\n57\t- **ICLR 2027**: Good fit — empirical analysis of evaluation methodology, psychometric rigor, large-scale characterization. Risk: ML reviewers may not be familiar with DIF/IRT. Mitigation: §3 provides self-contained DIF primer with ML translations.\n58\t- **Novelty concern**: DIF itself is not new (1988). Our contributions are: (1) the empirical finding of 38% instability, (2) the ground truth meta-critique affecting the field, (3) the semi-synthetic framework. Frame as \"bringing mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem.\"\n59\t\n60\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n61\t\n62\t1. **\"38% could just mean models got better\"** — Yes, partially. ρ=0.307 confirms correlation. We discuss this honestly. The contribution is the finding itself: benchmarks have massive item-level behavioral drift, regardless of cause.\n63\t\n64\t2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n65\t\n66\t3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n67\t\n68\t4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n69\t\n70\t5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n71\t\n72\t6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n73\t\n74\t## Dropped from Original Framing\n75\t\n76\t- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n77\t- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n78\t- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n79\t- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4252ca06-67da-4be4-a2cb-c5a7e1cf56da","uuid":"aebd61a0-de3c-477a-982c-9bf7fc78f06e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:53:58.749Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: ICLR 2027 (expected deadline ~October 2026)\n\n## Paper Type\nEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n\n## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: 38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This instability persists within every subject domain (within-subject C%=31-45%, avg 38.1%), is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02). This means cross-temporal MMLU score comparisons carry uncontrolled item-level variance.\n- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%, enrichment=0.96, sanity=0.18%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%)\n- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 means \"item instability\" may mainly reflect \"models got better at those domains\" — we cannot decompose 38% into contamination vs capability improvement\n\n**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\"\n\n**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n\n**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n2. **DIF items = discriminative signal carriers**: DIF removal causes significantly MORE ranking disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions). DIF items carry 1.09× cross-cohort accuracy swing vs 0.96× for stable items — they concentrate the information that differentiates model generations.\n3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587.\n4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF captures capability, not contamination**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005; partial r=−0.385 controlling accuracy; permutation p=0.014). Contaminated models (high GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma families) perform WORSE on DIF items. DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items that contamination cannot fake — contamination produces shallow performance gains concentrated on non-DIF items. N=33 matched models, leave-one-out robust (min |r|=0.374).\n- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 33 models)\n- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\" — mutual value positioning\n- Caveats (→ limitations): super-family analysis limited to N≥20 families; GSM1K validation N=33 (CI wide); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth\n\n## Story Spine\n\n**Problem**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Item-level behavioral drift across model generations could silently erode this assumption.\n\n**Approach**: We apply Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis — a classical psychometric tool for detecting item-level behavioral anomalies — to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n\n**What we find**: Three discoveries, each a contribution:\n\n1. **38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C DIF, temporal cohorts). This persists within every subject domain (31-45% per subject), is not statistical noise (random split: 0.18%), and cannot be eliminated by three structural corrections (matched-ability, IRT θ-conditioning, bifactor pre-check). The instability correlates with capability improvement (ρ=0.307) — models got better at those domains, changing how items function.\n\n2. **The field's standard validation approach has a blind spot**: Web-overlap contamination labels (Li et al.) measure whether an item is publicly accessible — a global, model-agnostic property. DIF detects whether an item functions differently across model cohorts — a dynamic, group-specific signal. Enrichment≈1.0 between DIF flags and web-overlap labels is expected when most items have equal global exposure (MMLU on Common Crawl since 2020). Semi-synthetic injection proves DIF works (ΔTPR=+0.508) — the problem is the ground truth, not the detector. Any contamination detection paper validating against web-overlap labels at the item level has the same issue.\n\n3. **A semi-synthetic validation framework** that injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices, measuring ΔTPR as the detection power metric. Cross-subject matching eliminates the matching variable contamination problem (ΔFPR=0.000). This infrastructure is reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n\n4. **DIF is a diagnostic lens, not a quality filter**. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after 31% removal), but DIF items concentrate the discriminative signal that differentiates model generations (removal disrupts rankings MORE than random, z=-8 to -49σ). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K confirms: DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination (r=−0.478, p=0.005) — contaminated models can't fake performance on DIF items, which capture genuine capability differences. DIF identifies which capability dimensions are changing — complementing Growing Pains' anchor-item approach by detecting which anchors are degrading.\n\n**Headline result**: \"Psychometric analysis of METABENCH reveals that 38% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations. Web-overlap contamination labels do not capture this differential signal (enrichment≈1.0), while semi-synthetic injection confirms detection capability (ΔTPR=+0.508 on MMLU, +0.818 on GSM8K). Cross-benchmark validation using GSM1K contamination gaps shows DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination (r=−0.478, p=0.005): DIF captures genuine capability-discriminative items that contamination cannot replicate. We provide a reusable validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000).\"\n\n## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n\nSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\n- **ICLR 2027**: Good fit — empirical analysis of evaluation methodology, psychometric rigor, large-scale characterization. Risk: ML reviewers may not be familiar with DIF/IRT. Mitigation: §3 provides self-contained DIF primer with ML translations.\n- **Novelty concern**: DIF itself is not new (1988). Our contributions are: (1) the empirical finding of 38% instability, (2) the ground truth meta-critique affecting the field, (3) the semi-synthetic framework. Frame as \"bringing mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n\n1. **\"38% could just mean models got better\"** — Yes, partially. ρ=0.307 confirms correlation. We discuss this honestly. The contribution is the finding itself: benchmarks have massive item-level behavioral drift, regardless of cause.\n\n2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n\n3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n\n4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n\n5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n\n6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n\n## Dropped from Original Framing\n\n- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T5GuDJxi2DAdhunNan99eU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Apply MH DIF to METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity analysis.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n7\t      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n8\t      \"temporal_enrichment\": 0.96,\n9\t      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n10\t      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n11\t      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n12\t      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n13\t      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n14\t      \"reviewer_interpretation\": \"capability_gain_detector\"\n15\t    },\n16\t    \"tables\": [\n17\t      {\n18\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary Under Worst Interpretation\",\n19\t        \"columns\": [\n20\t          \"cohort_definition\",\n21\t          \"c_pct\",\n22\t          \"enrichment_vs_li\",\n23\t          \"reviewer_counterargument\"\n24\t        ],\n25\t        \"rows\": [\n26\t          [\n27\t            \"temporal_2023_vs_2024\",\n28\t            31.3,\n29\t            0.96,\n30\t            \"Capability gain across generations\"\n31\t          ],\n32\t          [\n33\t            \"ability_quintile\",\n34\t            2.9,\n35\t            1.19,\n36\t            \"Minimal DIF = items function consistently across ability levels (expected)\"\n37\t          ],\n38\t          [\n39\t            \"within_family_llama2v3\",\n40\t            61.3,\n41\t            \"N/A\",\n42\t            \"Same family, same data pipeline → pure capability gain\"\n43\t          ],\n44\t          [\n45\t            \"random_split_sanity\",\n46\t            0.18,\n47\t            \"N/A\",\n48\t            \"Confirms real signal, but signal = capability\"\n49\t          ]\n50\t        ]\n51\t      },\n52\t      {\n53\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Domain Correlation Detail\",\n54\t        \"columns\": [\n55\t          \"metric\",\n56\t          \"value\",\n57\t          \"reviewer_spin\"\n58\t        ],\n59\t        \"rows\": [\n60\t          [\n61\t            \"spearman_rho_dif_vs_accuracy_gain\",\n62\t            0.307,\n63\t            \"DIF tracks capability improvement\"\n64\t          ],\n65\t          [\n66\t            \"p_value\",\n67\t            0.02,\n68\t            \"Significant correlation confirms confound\"\n69\t          ],\n70\t          [\n71\t            \"variance_explained\",\n72\t            \"9.4%\",\n73\t            \"Remainder is noise, not contamination signal\"\n74\t          ]\n75\t        ]\n76\t      }\n77\t    ],\n78\t    \"figures\": [],\n79\t    \"analysis\": \"The plan_001 results are empirically sound but narratively fatal under worst tier. The reframe salvages them: 31.3% \\\"instability\\\" becomes \\\"31% of items are capability- sensitive — they differentiate model generations.\\\" ρ=0.307 becomes evidence that DIF identifies items where genuine capability improvement concentrates. Enrichment=0.96 is reframed as \\\"DIF and contamination labels measure orthogonal constructs — DIF captures differential item functioning, not data leakage.\\\" The within-family result becomes the strongest evidence FOR the reframe: even within Llama family, DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest at the item level.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the actual results hold (these are already observed) but their interpretation collapses. ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) correlating DIF flags with domain-level accuracy improvement is seized by reviewers as causal evidence: items flagged by DIF are simply items where models improved. Enrichment=0.96 against Li et al. labels confirms zero relationship to contamination — reviewers read this as \\\"DIF doesn't detect contamination at all.\\\" The 61.3% within-family Llama-2-vs-3 C% is devastating: within the same family (same training pipeline, likely same data), DIF flags 61% of items, which reviewers attribute entirely to capability gain between generations. The sanity check (0.18%) proves DIF detects *something* real, but reviewers argue that \\\"something\\\" is capability improvement, not benchmark pathology. The meta-critique about Li et al. labels is dismissed: \\\"You haven't provided ANY ground truth showing DIF detects contamination — enrichment=0.96 is your only validation, and it failed.\\\"\\n\"\n80\t  },\n81\t  {\n82\t    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n83\t    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL training. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477) and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N≈13) cohorts. Compare DIF vs Min-K%/ConStat per-model degradation. Power analysis for partial exploitation.\\n\",\n84\t    \"results\": {\n85\t      \"n_reasoning_rl_models\": 13,\n86\t      \"dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.82,\n87\t      \"dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_reasoning_rl\": 0.71,\n88\t      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.63,\n89\t      \"mink_auroc_alignment_rl\": 0.58,\n90\t      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.44,\n91\t      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.67,\n92\t      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.51,\n93\t      \"dif_tpr_with_rl_in_cohort\": 0.65,\n94\t      \"dif_tpr_without_rl_in_cohort\": 0.68,\n95\t      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap\": true,\n96\t      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.23,\n97\t      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.34,\n98\t      \"partial_exploitation_detection_boundary\": 0.55,\n99\t      \"reviewer_score\": 4.0\n100\t    },\n101\t    \"tables\": [\n102\t      {\n103\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: RL Robustness Comparison (Underpowered)\",\n104\t        \"columns\": [\n105\t          \"method\",\n106\t          \"sft_auroc\",\n107\t          \"alignment_rl_auroc\",\n108\t          \"reasoning_rl_auroc\",\n109\t          \"95% CI width\"\n110\t        ],\n111\t        \"rows\": [\n112\t          [\n113\t            \"DIF_MH\",\n114\t            0.69,\n115\t            0.66,\n116\t            0.65,\n117\t            \"±0.14\"\n118\t          ],\n119\t          [\n120\t            \"Min-K%\",\n121\t            0.63,\n122\t            0.58,\n123\t            0.44,\n124\t            \"±0.18\"\n125\t          ],\n126\t          [\n127\t            \"ConStat\",\n128\t            0.67,\n129\t            0.61,\n130\t            0.51,\n131\t            \"±0.16\"\n132\t          ]\n133\t        ]\n134\t      },\n135\t      {\n136\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Statistical Tests (All Non-Significant)\",\n137\t        \"columns\": [\n138\t          \"comparison\",\n139\t          \"test\",\n140\t          \"statistic\",\n141\t          \"p_value\",\n142\t          \"conclusion\"\n143\t        ],\n144\t        \"rows\": [\n145\t          [\n146\t            \"DIF_vs_ConStat_RL\",\n147\t            \"DeLong\",\n148\t            \"z=1.19\",\n149\t            0.23,\n150\t            \"Cannot reject H0\"\n151\t          ],\n152\t          [\n153\t            \"DIF_retention_equivalence\",\n154\t            \"TOST\",\n155\t            \"t=0.96\",\n156\t            0.34,\n157\t            \"Cannot claim equivalence\"\n158\t          ],\n159\t          [\n160\t            \"flag_overlap_SFT_vs_RL\",\n161\t            \"Bootstrap Jaccard\",\n162\t            0.71,\n163\t            \"CI [0.54, 0.88]\",\n164\t            \"Wide CI, inconclusive\"\n165\t          ]\n166\t        ]\n167\t      }\n168\t    ],\n169\t    \"figures\": [],\n170\t    \"analysis\": \"Experiment is fatally underpowered. N=13 reasoning-RL models cannot support any statistical claim. Under worst tier, this becomes ammunition: \\\"authors claim RL robustness but provide no evidence.\\\" Reframe option: drop RL robustness as a claim entirely, acknowledge in limitations, reposition the alignment-RL (N=477) result as \\\"DIF is stable across training objective variations\\\" without claiming reasoning-RL coverage. Under the capability-archaeology reframe, RL robustness is less central anyway — the question becomes \\\"do capability-authentic items remain stable across training paradigms?\\\" which is interesting but not load-bearing.\\n\\n\\nWith only N=13 reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical significance. DIF flag overlap between SFT and RL-containing cohorts is 71%, which reviewers dismiss as \\\"you can't distinguish robustness from noise with N=13.\\\" The DeLong test p=0.23 means we cannot claim DIF outperforms ConStat. TOST equivalence test fails (p=0.34 > 0.05) — we can't even claim equivalence. Min-K% drops to 0.44 AUROC on reasoning-RL models, but reviewers note \\\"you're comparing DIF (group-level, N=477 cohort) against per-model detectors on 13 models — apples to oranges.\\\" The partial exploitation analysis shows detection boundary at p=0.55, meaning DIF misses contamination below 55% exploitation rate — reviewers call this \\\"DIF only detects extreme contamination, which is also detectable by simpler methods.\\\" The entire experiment is underpowered and reviewers recommend \\\"defer until more GRPO/PPO models exist on public leaderboards.\\\"\\n\"\n171\t  },\n172\t  {\n173\t    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n174\t    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on 200+ MMLU items. Generate semantic reformulations, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n175\t    \"results\": {\n176\t      \"n_items_reformulated\": 220,\n177\t      \"n_models_tested\": 15,\n178\t      \"constat_detection_rate_non_rl\": 0.41,\n179\t      \"constat_detection_rate_rl\": 0.12,\n180\t      \"constat_fdr_controlled_rate\": 0.28,\n181\t      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap\": 0.18,\n182\t      \"paraphrase_quality_score\": 0.73,\n183\t      \"reformulation_difficulty_shift_mean\": 0.08,\n184\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"paraphrase quality uncontrolled\"\n185\t    },\n186\t    \"tables\": [\n187\t      {\n188\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: ConStat vs DIF Detection Comparison\",\n189\t        \"columns\": [\n190\t          \"method\",\n191\t          \"detection_target\",\n192\t          \"rate_non_rl\",\n193\t          \"rate_rl\",\n194\t          \"per_model\",\n195\t          \"requires_cohort\"\n196\t        ],\n197\t        \"rows\": [\n198\t          [\n199\t            \"ConStat\",\n200\t            \"memorization\",\n201\t            0.41,\n202\t            0.12,\n203\t            \"Yes\",\n204\t            \"No\"\n205\t          ],\n206\t          [\n207\t            \"DIF_MH\",\n208\t            \"differential_functioning\",\n209\t            0.313,\n210\t            \"N/A (group-level)\",\n211\t            \"No\",\n212\t            \"Yes\"\n213\t          ],\n214\t          [\n215\t            \"overlap\",\n216\t            \"both_flag\",\n217\t            0.18,\n218\t            \"N/A\",\n219\t            \"-\",\n220\t            \"-\"\n221\t          ]\n222\t        ]\n223\t      }\n224\t    ],\n225\t    \"figures\": [],\n226\t    \"analysis\": \"ConStat comparison backfires under worst tier. It provides a foil that makes DIF look less like a contamination detector. Under reframe, this becomes irrelevant — DIF is repositioned as capability-item identifier, not competing with ConStat on contamination detection. Drop from reframed paper or move to appendix as \\\"related method comparison showing orthogonal signals.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, ConStat shows moderate detection on non-RL models (41%) — notably higher than DIF's enrichment=0.96 against contamination labels. Reviewers argue \\\"ConStat at least detects something correlated with memorization; DIF detects capability improvement.\\\" The 18% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags confirms they measure different constructs, but reviewers spin this negatively: \\\"DIF is orthogonal to contamination detection.\\\" The RL degradation (41%→12%) is expected but reviewers note \\\"at least ConStat provides per-model detection; DIF requires cohort structure.\\\" Paraphrase quality (0.73) is attacked: \\\"reformulations may introduce difficulty shifts (mean Δ=0.08), confounding detection.\\\" The entire comparison undermines DIF rather than supporting it — ConStat looks like the more direct contamination tool, and DIF looks like a capability change detector with no contamination detection ability.\\n\"\n227\t  },\n228\t  {\n229\t    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n230\t    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance evaluation on 10-15 open-weight models. 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection for contamination.\\n\",\n231\t    \"results\": {\n232\t      \"n_models\": 12,\n233\t      \"n_items_tested\": 500,\n234\t      \"dvd_bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.23,\n235\t      \"dvd_variance_auc\": 0.58,\n236\t      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap\": 0.14,\n237\t      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"high (results vary 15-30% across T=0.7 vs T=1.0)\",\n238\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 65,\n239\t      \"generation_cost_barrier\": \"high\",\n240\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"expensive and noisy\"\n241\t    },\n242\t    \"tables\": [\n243\t      {\n244\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: DVD Results Summary\",\n245\t        \"columns\": [\n246\t          \"metric\",\n247\t          \"value\",\n248\t          \"stability\"\n249\t        ],\n250\t        \"rows\": [\n251\t          [\n252\t            \"bimodal_detection_rate\",\n253\t            0.23,\n254\t            \"±0.09 across temperatures\"\n255\t          ],\n256\t          [\n257\t            \"variance_auc\",\n258\t            0.58,\n259\t            \"±0.07\"\n260\t          ],\n261\t          [\n262\t            \"dif_overlap\",\n263\t            0.14,\n264\t            \"stable\"\n265\t          ],\n266\t          [\n267\t            \"cost_per_model\",\n268\t            \"5.4 GPU-hrs\",\n269\t            \"fixed\"\n270\t          ]\n271\t        ]\n272\t      }\n273\t    ],\n274\t    \"figures\": [],\n275\t    \"analysis\": \"DVD is the weakest experiment under worst tier. Low AUC, high cost, temperature sensitivity, minimal DIF overlap. Under reframe, DVD is irrelevant — drop entirely. The capability-archaeology framing doesn't need generation variance.\\n\\n\\nDVD shows weak detection (AUC=0.58, barely above chance) on only 12 models. The bimodal detection rate of 23% is unstable across temperature settings (15-30% variation), making the signal unreliable. Overlap with DIF is minimal (14%), confirming both methods detect different phenomena. Under worst tier, reviewers dismiss DVD as \\\"noisy, expensive, and only applicable to open-weight models.\\\" The 65 GPU-hour cost for testing 12 models × 500 items is criticized as impractical. More importantly, DVD's failure doesn't help DIF's case — reviewers note \\\"neither DVD nor DIF convincingly detects contamination; DIF detects capability change, DVD detects generation instability, neither detects what we actually care about.\\\" The experiment consumes resources without strengthening any claim.\\n\"\n276\t  },\n277\t  {\n278\t    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n279\t    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting and weighted fusion. Cross-validate fusion weights.\\n\",\n280\t    \"results\": {\n281\t      \"ensemble_2of3_precision\": 0.31,\n282\t      \"ensemble_2of3_recall\": 0.15,\n283\t      \"ensemble_weighted_auc\": 0.62,\n284\t      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.14,\n285\t      \"uniform_weight_auc\": 0.59,\n286\t      \"optimized_weight_auc\": 0.64,\n287\t      \"cv_overfit_gap\": 0.08,\n288\t      \"dif_weight_in_optimized\": 0.22,\n289\t      \"constat_weight_in_optimized\": 0.51,\n290\t      \"dvd_weight_in_optimized\": 0.27,\n291\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"ensemble of weak signals is still weak\"\n292\t    },\n293\t    \"tables\": [\n294\t      {\n295\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Ensemble Performance (Worst Tier)\",\n296\t        \"columns\": [\n297\t          \"strategy\",\n298\t          \"precision\",\n299\t          \"recall\",\n300\t          \"auc\",\n301\t          \"fpr\"\n302\t        ],\n303\t        \"rows\": [\n304\t          [\n305\t            \"DIF_only\",\n306\t            0.29,\n307\t            0.31,\n308\t            0.54,\n309\t            0.31\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"ConStat_only\",\n313\t            0.38,\n314\t            0.41,\n315\t            0.61,\n316\t            0.22\n317\t          ],\n318\t          [\n319\t            \"DVD_only\",\n320\t            0.24,\n321\t            0.23,\n322\t            0.58,\n323\t            0.28\n324\t          ],\n325\t          [\n326\t            \"majority_2of3\",\n327\t            0.31,\n328\t            0.15,\n329\t            0.55,\n330\t            0.14\n331\t          ],\n332\t          [\n333\t            \"weighted_fusion_cv\",\n334\t            0.35,\n335\t            0.32,\n336\t            0.64,\n337\t            0.19\n338\t          ]\n339\t        ]\n340\t      }\n341\t    ],\n342\t    \"figures\": [],\n343\t    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble experiment is counterproductive under worst tier. It demonstrates DIF's weakness relative to ConStat for contamination detection. Under reframe, the entire plan_003 sequence should be dropped — the capability-archaeology paper doesn't need contamination detection ensemble. Save 150 GPU-hours.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, ensemble fusion fails to produce meaningful improvement. The 2-of-3 majority voting achieves only 15% recall because DIF, ConStat, and DVD flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14-0.18). Weighted fusion (AUC=0.64) barely beats uniform (0.59), and cross-validation reveals 0.08 overfit gap, suggesting the optimized weights don't generalize. Critically, DIF receives the lowest weight (0.22) in the optimized ensemble — reviewers read this as \\\"even the ensemble agrees DIF contributes least to contamination detection.\\\" The ensemble FPR of 0.14 technically meets the <0.10 target... no, it doesn't (0.14 > 0.10). Reviewers conclude: \\\"three individually weak contamination signals don't combine into a strong one. The fundamental problem — lack of ground truth — isn't solved by fusion.\\\" The entire plan_003 sequence (a+b+c) consumed 150 GPU-hours for a negative meta-result.\\n\"\n344\t  },\n345\t  {\n346\t    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n347\t    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation as external validation.\\n\",\n348\t    \"results\": {\n349\t      \"gsm8k_temporal_c_pct\": 18.9,\n350\t      \"gsm8k_semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n351\t      \"gsm1k_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n352\t      \"gsm1k_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n353\t      \"gsm1k_partial_r\": -0.385,\n354\t      \"gsm1k_n_models\": 33,\n355\t      \"gsm1k_bootstrap_ci_r\": [\n356\t        -0.71,\n357\t        -0.12\n358\t      ],\n359\t      \"gsm1k_permutation_p\": 0.014,\n360\t      \"cook_d_max\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n361\t      \"leave_one_out_min_r\": -0.374,\n362\t      \"reviewer_reinterpretation\": \"anti-correlation confirms DIF=capability, not contamination\"\n363\t    },\n364\t    \"tables\": [\n365\t      {\n366\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: GSM1K Correlation Under Worst-Case Scrutiny\",\n367\t        \"columns\": [\n368\t          \"metric\",\n369\t          \"point_estimate\",\n370\t          \"95% CI\",\n371\t          \"reviewer_counterargument\"\n372\t        ],\n373\t        \"rows\": [\n374\t          [\n375\t            \"pearson_r\",\n376\t            -0.478,\n377\t            \"[-0.71, -0.12]\",\n378\t            \"CI includes near-zero; N=33 too small\"\n379\t          ],\n380\t          [\n381\t            \"partial_r\",\n382\t            -0.385,\n383\t            \"[-0.64, -0.04]\",\n384\t            \"Controlling accuracy weakens signal\"\n385\t          ],\n386\t          [\n387\t            \"permutation_p\",\n388\t            0.014,\n389\t            \"N/A\",\n390\t            \"Multiple testing not corrected\"\n391\t          ],\n392\t          [\n393\t            \"leave_one_out_min\",\n394\t            -0.374,\n395\t            \"N/A\",\n396\t            \"One model removal cuts 22% of effect\"\n397\t          ]\n398\t        ]\n399\t      },\n400\t      {\n401\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Cross-Benchmark C% Instability\",\n402\t        \"columns\": [\n403\t          \"benchmark\",\n404\t          \"temporal_c_pct\",\n405\t          \"interpretation\"\n406\t        ],\n407\t        \"rows\": [\n408\t          [\n409\t            \"MMLU\",\n410\t            31.3,\n411\t            \"High instability or high capability sensitivity\"\n412\t          ],\n413\t          [\n414\t            \"GSM8K\",\n415\t            18.9,\n416\t            \"Lower instability — benchmark-dependent\"\n417\t          ],\n418\t          [\n419\t            \"delta\",\n420\t            12.4,\n421\t            \"No universal 'instability rate' — DIF is benchmark-specific\"\n422\t          ]\n423\t        ]\n424\t      }\n425\t    ],\n426\t    \"figures\": [],\n427\t    \"analysis\": \"The GSM1K correlation is the pivotal result. Under original framing, r=−0.478 is devastating (DIF anti-correlates with contamination). Under reframe, it becomes the HEADLINE: \\\"DIF identifies capability-authentic items that contamination cannot replicate.\\\" The wide CI (N=33) remains a vulnerability. The reframed paper should lead with this finding and frame it as: \\\"Contaminated models show inflated scores on non-DIF items (shallow memorization) but cannot maintain performance on DIF items (genuine capability discriminators).\\\" ΔTPR=0.818 is repositioned as \\\"calibration infrastructure\\\" rather than \\\"detection proof.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the r=−0.478 result — the strongest cross-benchmark finding — is turned against the original narrative. Reviewers argue: \\\"DIF items anti-correlate with contamination gap. Models that are MORE contaminated (high GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma) perform WORSE on DIF items. This proves DIF identifies items where genuine capability matters — contaminated models can't fake their way through these items. DIF is a capability detector, not a contamination detector.\\\" The bootstrap CI [-0.71, -0.12] is wide (N=33), and reviewers note \\\"this could be as weak as r=-0.12, barely meaningful.\\\" The ΔTPR=0.818 on semi-synthetic injection is dismissed: \\\"synthetic contamination (flipping incorrect→correct deterministically) doesn't resemble real contamination (partial memorization, shallow pattern matching). High ΔTPR on synthetic data doesn't validate real-world detection.\\\" The 18.9% temporal C% on GSM8K (vs 31.3% on MMLU) is spun as \\\"DIF flags vary wildly across benchmarks — no stable characterization.\\\" Deepseek-math-7b-rl as Cook's D outlier raises concerns about robustness of the correlation despite leave-one-out showing min |r|=0.374.\\n\"\n428\t  },\n429\t  {\n430\t    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n431\t    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT on MMLU (general factor + 57 subject factors). Anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration for replacement items.\\n\",\n432\t    \"results\": {\n433\t      \"bifactor_convergence\": false,\n434\t      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.11,\n435\t      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.72,\n436\t      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.68,\n437\t      \"n_subjects_57\": 57,\n438\t      \"n_items_12508\": 12508,\n439\t      \"n_models_5219\": 5219,\n440\t      \"anchor_items_selected\": 0,\n441\t      \"equating_rho\": \"N/A (model failed to converge)\",\n442\t      \"computation_time_hours\": 48,\n443\t      \"memory_peak_gb\": 180,\n444\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"57-factor bifactor on 12K items is computationally intractable\"\n445\t    },\n446\t    \"tables\": [\n447\t      {\n448\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Bifactor IRT Fit Diagnostics\",\n449\t        \"columns\": [\n450\t          \"metric\",\n451\t          \"value\",\n452\t          \"threshold\",\n453\t          \"verdict\"\n454\t        ],\n455\t        \"rows\": [\n456\t          [\n457\t            \"RMSEA\",\n458\t            0.11,\n459\t            \"<0.08\",\n460\t            \"FAIL\"\n461\t          ],\n462\t          [\n463\t            \"CFI\",\n464\t            0.72,\n465\t            \">0.90\",\n466\t            \"FAIL\"\n467\t          ],\n468\t          [\n469\t            \"TLI\",\n470\t            0.68,\n471\t            \">0.90\",\n472\t            \"FAIL\"\n473\t          ],\n474\t          [\n475\t            \"SRMR\",\n476\t            0.09,\n477\t            \"<0.08\",\n478\t            \"FAIL\"\n479\t          ],\n480\t          [\n481\t            \"convergence\",\n482\t            \"No (48h timeout)\",\n483\t            \"Yes\",\n484\t            \"FAIL\"\n485\t          ],\n486\t          [\n487\t            \"n_parameters\",\n488\t            \"~25,000\",\n489\t            \"<5,000 practical\",\n490\t            \"FAIL\"\n491\t          ]\n492\t        ]\n493\t      }\n494\t    ],\n495\t    \"figures\": [],\n496\t    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor failure is expected and, under worst tier, becomes evidence that MMLU is fundamentally unsuitable for IRT-based psychometric analysis. Reviewers cite this as proof that \\\"applying unidimensional psychometric methods to a 57-subject multidimensional benchmark is methodologically invalid.\\\" Under reframe, this is acknowledged honestly in limitations: \\\"MMLU's multidimensional structure exceeds current IRT modeling capacity; our within-subject analysis (plan_014) partially addresses this but doesn't eliminate the concern.\\\" Drop the equating pipeline entirely.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, bifactor IRT fails catastrophically. The 57-subject structure of MMLU means a bifactor model requires estimating 1 general + 57 specific factors — a 58-dimensional latent space with ~25,000 item parameters. This exceeds practical computational limits (180GB RAM, 48 hours without convergence). Even if forced to converge with simplifications (e.g., constraining factor loadings), fit indices are poor: RMSEA=0.11 (>0.08 threshold), CFI=0.72 (<0.90 threshold), TLI=0.68 (<0.90). The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is confirmed as violated, but the alternative (bifactor) is intractable. Reviewers argue: \\\"You've demonstrated that the psychometric foundation of your method is inappropriate for MMLU, but provided no working alternative. Multidimensional DIF methods exist (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) but you haven't implemented them.\\\" Anchor item selection fails because the bifactor model didn't converge — no equating is possible. The entire regeneration pipeline (plan_005 → plan_006 → plan_008) collapses at step one.\\n\"\n497\t  },\n498\t  {\n499\t    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n500\t    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. LLM generation with construct constraints, student simulation calibration, difficulty correlation validation.\\n\",\n501\t    \"results\": {\n502\t      \"n_items_generated\": 300,\n503\t      \"n_subjects_covered\": 15,\n504\t      \"difficulty_correlation_prompt\": 0.18,\n505\t      \"difficulty_correlation_student_sim\": 0.34,\n506\t      \"cognitive_level_match_rate\": 0.45,\n507\t      \"item_quality_human_eval\": 0.61,\n508\t      \"construct_alignment_score\": 0.53,\n509\t      \"holdout_panel_rho\": 0.42,\n510\t      \"gpu_hours_used\": 180,\n511\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"difficulty calibration too weak for operational use\"\n512\t    },\n513\t    \"tables\": [\n514\t      {\n515\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Item Generation Calibration Failure\",\n516\t        \"columns\": [\n517\t          \"calibration_method\",\n518\t          \"difficulty_rho\",\n519\t          \"claimed_in_literature\",\n520\t          \"achieved\"\n521\t        ],\n522\t        \"rows\": [\n523\t          [\n524\t            \"prompt_targeting\",\n525\t            0.18,\n526\t            \"0.10-0.45\",\n527\t            \"Low end\"\n528\t          ],\n529\t          [\n530\t            \"student_simulation\",\n531\t            0.34,\n532\t            \"0.75-0.82\",\n533\t            \"Far below\"\n534\t          ],\n535\t          [\n536\t            \"holdout_validation\",\n537\t            0.42,\n538\t            \">0.90 needed\",\n539\t            \"Insufficient\"\n540\t          ]\n541\t        ]\n542\t      }\n543\t    ],\n544\t    \"figures\": [],\n545\t    \"analysis\": \"Item generation is the most expensive failure (180 GPU-hrs). Under worst tier, it actively undermines the paper — reviewers use it to argue \\\"you can't replace DIF items, and you've shown DIF items don't affect rankings (ρ=0.997), so what's the practical value?\\\" Under reframe, this experiment should not be run at all. The capability-archaeology paper frames DIF items as valuable signal carriers, not problems to be replaced. Save the 200 GPU-hour budget.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, item generation produces mediocre results. Prompt-based difficulty targeting yields ρ=0.18 (consistent with literature range 0.10-0.45 from arXiv 2602.06631 — on the low end). Student simulation calibration improves to ρ=0.34 but still far below the r=0.75-0.82 claimed in arXiv 2601.09953 — the discrepancy arises because student simulation was validated on simple math items, not MMLU's diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45%) means over half of replacement items test different cognitive processes than originals. Construct alignment (53%) is poor — reviewers argue \\\"replacing items with construct-misaligned substitutes invalidates the benchmark more than the DIF items did.\\\" The holdout panel validation (ρ=0.42) shows replacement items don't preserve model rankings. Reviewers conclude: \\\"The cure is worse than the disease — DIF-flagged items at least produce stable rankings (ρ=0.997); replacements introduce genuine measurement error.\\\" 180 GPU-hours consumed for a negative result. Under reframe, this experiment is dropped entirely — the capability-archaeology paper doesn't need item replacement.\\n\"\n546\t  },\n547\t  {\n548\t    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n549\t    \"description\": \"Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006). Simulate 4 successive administrations. Show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\\n\",\n550\t    \"results\": {\n551\t      \"single_admin_power_d10\": 0.38,\n552\t      \"cumulative_2_admin_power_d10\": 0.49,\n553\t      \"cumulative_3_admin_power_d10\": 0.58,\n554\t      \"cumulative_4_admin_power_d10\": 0.64,\n555\t      \"single_admin_fpr\": 0.05,\n556\t      \"cumulative_4_admin_fpr\": 0.11,\n557\t      \"fpr_inflation_per_admin\": 0.02,\n558\t      \"convergence_rate\": 0.92,\n559\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"cumulative FPR inflation makes this impractical\"\n560\t    },\n561\t    \"tables\": [\n562\t      {\n563\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Trajectory\",\n564\t        \"columns\": [\n565\t          \"n_administrations\",\n566\t          \"tpr_d10\",\n567\t          \"tpr_d14\",\n568\t          \"fpr\",\n569\t          \"net_power_gain\"\n570\t        ],\n571\t        \"rows\": [\n572\t          [\n573\t            1,\n574\t            0.38,\n575\t            0.68,\n576\t            0.05,\n577\t            \"baseline\"\n578\t          ],\n579\t          [\n580\t            2,\n581\t            0.49,\n582\t            0.78,\n583\t            0.07,\n584\t            \"+0.11/+0.10\"\n585\t          ],\n586\t          [\n587\t            3,\n588\t            0.58,\n589\t            0.84,\n590\t            0.09,\n591\t            \"+0.09/+0.06\"\n592\t          ],\n593\t          [\n594\t            4,\n595\t            0.64,\n596\t            0.87,\n597\t            0.11,\n598\t            \"+0.06/+0.03\"\n599\t          ]\n600\t        ]\n601\t      }\n602\t    ],\n603\t    \"figures\": [],\n604\t    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian is theoretically elegant but practically undermined by FPR inflation. Under worst tier, it's a distraction — reviewers see diminishing returns and growing false positives. Under reframe, it has modest value as \\\"longitudinal capability tracking infrastructure\\\" but is not central. Recommend moving to appendix or future work.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows power improvement (0.38→0.64 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) but fails to reach the 0.70 target from the plan description. More critically, FPR inflates from 0.05 to 0.11 across 4 administrations — each administration's prior carries forward false positive evidence. Reviewers argue: \\\"With baseline FPR already at 31% in real data (plan_001), cumulative approaches will amplify false positives. You're accumulating evidence that items changed function — but we've established this is capability improvement, not contamination. You're building a more powerful detector of the wrong thing.\\\" The simulation uses idealized conditions (known item parameters, controlled drift) that don't match real MMLU conditions. Under reframe, this becomes interesting — \\\"cumulative tracking of capability-sensitive items across model generations\\\" — but the FPR inflation concern remains.\\n\"\n605\t  },\n606\t  {\n607\t    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n608\t    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement (5%-50%). Validity metrics, theoretical bounds, empirical validation.\\n\",\n609\t    \"results\": {\n610\t      \"rho_at_5pct_replacement\": 0.998,\n611\t      \"rho_at_10pct_replacement\": 0.996,\n612\t      \"rho_at_20pct_replacement\": 0.991,\n613\t      \"rho_at_50pct_replacement\": 0.972,\n614\t      \"reliability_alpha_original\": 0.94,\n615\t      \"reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced\": 0.88,\n616\t      \"construct_coverage_original\": 57,\n617\t      \"construct_coverage_50pct_replaced\": 52,\n618\t      \"theoretical_bound_rho_min\": 0.95,\n619\t      \"replacement_rate_at_bound\": 0.38,\n620\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"confirms DIF removal doesn't matter\"\n621\t    },\n622\t    \"tables\": [\n623\t      {\n624\t        \"name\": \"Table 13: Graceful Degradation Curves\",\n625\t        \"columns\": [\n626\t          \"replacement_pct\",\n627\t          \"rank_rho\",\n628\t          \"reliability_alpha\",\n629\t          \"subjects_covered\",\n630\t          \"verdict\"\n631\t        ],\n632\t        \"rows\": [\n633\t          [\n634\t            0,\n635\t            1.0,\n636\t            0.94,\n637\t            57,\n638\t            \"baseline\"\n639\t          ],\n640\t          [\n641\t            5,\n642\t            0.998,\n643\t            0.93,\n644\t            57,\n645\t            \"negligible\"\n646\t          ],\n647\t          [\n648\t            10,\n649\t            0.996,\n650\t            0.93,\n651\t            57,\n652\t            \"negligible\"\n653\t          ],\n654\t          [\n655\t            20,\n656\t            0.991,\n657\t            0.91,\n658\t            56,\n659\t            \"minor\"\n660\t          ],\n661\t          [\n662\t            31,\n663\t            0.987,\n664\t            0.9,\n665\t            55,\n666\t            \"DIF removal point\"\n667\t          ],\n668\t          [\n669\t            50,\n670\t            0.972,\n671\t            0.88,\n672\t            52,\n673\t            \"still acceptable\"\n674\t          ]\n675\t        ]\n676\t      }\n677\t    ],\n678\t    \"figures\": [],\n679\t    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation is self-defeating under worst tier AND original framing. It proves removing DIF items doesn't matter, undermining significance. Under reframe, this becomes: \\\"MMLU is 3× larger than needed for stable rankings — DIF identifies the informative core (the items that DO contribute discriminative signal).\\\" The ρ=0.972 at 50% replacement becomes \\\"you could cut MMLU in half and preserve rankings — DIF tells you which half to keep.\\\" This is actually a strong reframe, turning the worst finding into a measurement efficiency contribution.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, graceful degradation analysis confirms the ρ=0.997 finding from plan_017 — removing/replacing up to 50% of items barely affects rankings (ρ=0.972). Reviewers weaponize this: \\\"Even replacing HALF the benchmark changes rankings by less than 3%. This proves MMLU is massively redundant, and DIF-flagged items are redundant too. Why should anyone care about 31% item instability if it doesn't affect rankings?\\\" Reliability drops from 0.94 to 0.88 at 50% replacement, but remains above acceptable thresholds (0.70). Construct coverage loses 5 of 57 subjects at 50% replacement — reviewers note \\\"acceptable.\\\" The theoretical bound (ρ≥0.95 up to 38% replacement) perfectly matches the DIF flagging rate (31.3%), which reviewers read as \\\"you can safely ignore everything DIF flags.\\\" This experiment depends on plan_005/006 (which fail), so the \\\"replacement\\\" is simulated via random item substitution rather than actual generated items — further reducing credibility.\\n\"\n680\t  },\n681\t  {\n682\t    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n683\t    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical cohort DIF. Continuous covariate (release date). Compare detection power vs categorical MH.\\n\",\n684\t    \"results\": {\n685\t      \"ift_detection_rate\": 0.34,\n686\t      \"ift_vs_mh_overlap\": 0.78,\n687\t      \"ift_unique_flags\": 0.06,\n688\t      \"mh_unique_flags\": 0.04,\n689\t      \"ift_uses_full_sample\": true,\n690\t      \"ift_avoids_cohort_splits\": true,\n691\t      \"ift_computational_cost_ratio\": 12.0,\n692\t      \"ift_release_date_coefficient_mean\": 0.08,\n693\t      \"ift_release_date_coefficient_std\": 0.15,\n694\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"confirms temporal trend = capability gain\"\n695\t    },\n696\t    \"tables\": [\n697\t      {\n698\t        \"name\": \"Table 14: IFT vs MH Comparison\",\n699\t        \"columns\": [\n700\t          \"method\",\n701\t          \"flagging_rate\",\n702\t          \"unique_flags\",\n703\t          \"cost_ratio\",\n704\t          \"temporal_pattern\"\n705\t        ],\n706\t        \"rows\": [\n707\t          [\n708\t            \"MH_categorical\",\n709\t            0.313,\n710\t            0.04,\n711\t            1.0,\n712\t            \"Discrete cohort split\"\n713\t          ],\n714\t          [\n715\t            \"IFT_continuous\",\n716\t            0.34,\n717\t            0.06,\n718\t            12.0,\n719\t            \"Smooth positive trend\"\n720\t          ],\n721\t          [\n722\t            \"overlap\",\n723\t            0.78,\n724\t            \"-\",\n725\t            \"-\",\n726\t            \"Same underlying signal\"\n727\t          ]\n728\t        ]\n729\t      }\n730\t    ],\n731\t    \"figures\": [],\n732\t    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides marginal methodological value but under worst tier confirms the capability-improvement interpretation via smooth temporal trends. Under reframe, the smooth trend is actually interesting: \\\"capability-authentic items show predictable sensitivity to model generation — enabling prospective identification of items that will become less discriminative.\\\" Recommend as supplementary/appendix rather than main paper contribution.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 34% of items as having significant temporal DIF — nearly identical to MH's 31.3%. The 78% overlap confirms both methods detect the same phenomenon. The IFT release-date coefficient (mean=0.08, SD=0.15) is positive: items become easier over time, consistent with capability improvement. Reviewers read the continuous coefficient as: \\\"DIF is a smooth function of time, not a discrete shift — this is exactly what capability improvement looks like. If contamination caused DIF, we'd expect discrete jumps when specific training data became available.\\\" The 12× computational cost over MH makes IFT impractical at scale. Reviewers conclude: \\\"IFT confirms MH findings but adds computational cost and the interpretation that item changes are gradual (capability) not abrupt (contamination).\\\"\\n\"\n733\t  },\n734\t  {\n735\t    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n736\t    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only homogeneous subpopulation. IRT fit diagnostics. Compare FPR/TPR vs full-population DIF from plan_001.\\n\",\n737\t    \"results\": {\n738\t      \"n_decoder_only\": 3400,\n739\t      \"decoder_only_c_pct\": 33.1,\n740\t      \"decoder_only_irt_rmsea\": 0.092,\n741\t      \"decoder_only_irt_cfi\": 0.81,\n742\t      \"decoder_only_irt_tli\": 0.78,\n743\t      \"decoder_only_irt_srmr\": 0.073,\n744\t      \"full_population_c_pct\": 31.3,\n745\t      \"delta_c_pct\": 1.8,\n746\t      \"fpr_decoder_only\": 0.33,\n747\t      \"fpr_full_population\": 0.318,\n748\t      \"tpr_delta_p05\": -0.02,\n749\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"architecture homogeneity doesn't fix the problem\"\n750\t    },\n751\t    \"tables\": [\n752\t      {\n753\t        \"name\": \"Table 15: Architecture Stratification Results\",\n754\t        \"columns\": [\n755\t          \"population\",\n756\t          \"n_models\",\n757\t          \"c_pct\",\n758\t          \"rmsea\",\n759\t          \"cfi\",\n760\t          \"tli\"\n761\t        ],\n762\t        \"rows\": [\n763\t          [\n764\t            \"full_population\",\n765\t            5219,\n766\t            31.3,\n767\t            \"N/A (not computed)\",\n768\t            \"N/A\",\n769\t            \"N/A\"\n770\t          ],\n771\t          [\n772\t            \"decoder_only\",\n773\t            3400,\n774\t            33.1,\n775\t            0.092,\n776\t            0.81,\n777\t            0.78\n778\t          ],\n779\t          [\n780\t            \"delta\",\n781\t            \"-1819\",\n782\t            \"+1.8\",\n783\t            \"FAIL (>0.08)\",\n784\t            \"FAIL (<0.90)\",\n785\t            \"FAIL (<0.90)\"\n786\t          ]\n787\t        ]\n788\t      },\n789\t      {\n790\t        \"name\": \"Table 16: Detection Power Comparison\",\n791\t        \"columns\": [\n792\t          \"population\",\n793\t          \"fpr_baseline\",\n794\t          \"tpr_p05\",\n795\t          \"tpr_p10\",\n796\t          \"enrichment\"\n797\t        ],\n798\t        \"rows\": [\n799\t          [\n800\t            \"full_population\",\n801\t            0.318,\n802\t            0.834,\n803\t            1.0,\n804\t            0.96\n805\t          ],\n806\t          [\n807\t            \"decoder_only\",\n808\t            0.33,\n809\t            0.82,\n810\t            0.99,\n811\t            0.95\n812\t          ],\n813\t          [\n814\t            \"delta\",\n815\t            \"+0.012\",\n816\t            \"-0.014\",\n817\t            \"-0.01\",\n818\t            \"-0.01\"\n819\t          ]\n820\t        ]\n821\t      }\n822\t    ],\n823\t    \"figures\": [],\n824\t    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification is the critical missing experiment identified by worst-case Dream, and it produces the predicted negative result. Under worst tier, this closes the last escape route: DIF flagging rates cannot be reduced by controlling for architecture. The IRT misfit is structural (MMLU dimensionality) not population-driven. Under reframe, this becomes neutral: \\\"DIF item sensitivity is robust to population composition — capability-authentic items are identified regardless of architecture mix.\\\" The IRT fit concern is acknowledged honestly but framed as \\\"conservative: poor IRT fit means DIF may UNDERESTIMATE the true proportion of capability-sensitive items.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier (explicitly specified in tier definition), architecture stratification fails to improve results. Decoder-only C% = 33.1% (vs 31.3% full population) — no meaningful reduction, slightly HIGHER. IRT fit remains poor: RMSEA=0.092 (>0.08), CFI=0.81 (<0.90), TLI=0.78 (<0.90). Removing encoder-decoder and hybrid architectures doesn't address the fundamental unidimensionality problem — MMLU's 57-subject structure violates unidimensional IRT regardless of architecture homogeneity. The C% increase from 31.3%→33.1% is within noise but reviewers spin it: \\\"architecture stratification made things WORSE — DIF flags more items in a homogeneous population, confirming that flags reflect capability variation, not architecture confounding.\\\" FPR and TPR are essentially unchanged (ΔFPR=+0.01, ΔTPR=−0.02). Reviewers familiar with psychometrics argue: \\\"RMSEA=0.092 on decoder-only models proves the IRT model is misspecified for MMLU. Your entire DIF analysis rests on an inappropriate measurement model. This is not fixable by subsetting — it requires multidimensional IRT, which you haven't implemented.\\\"\\n\"\n825\t  },\n826\t  {\n827\t    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n828\t    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix. Measure DIF TPR/FPR against controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\\n\",\n829\t    \"results\": {\n830\t      \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.222,\n831\t      \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n832\t      \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.664,\n833\t      \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n834\t      \"external_matching_fpr\": 0.314,\n835\t      \"within_matching_fpr_inflation\": 0.186,\n836\t      \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n837\t      \"cross_subject_delta_tpr_p05_avg\": 0.36,\n838\t      \"dose_response_present\": true,\n839\t      \"reviewer_critique\": \"synthetic injection mechanism unrealistic\"\n840\t    },\n841\t    \"tables\": [\n842\t      {\n843\t        \"name\": \"Table 17: Semi-Synthetic Results Under Worst Interpretation\",\n844\t        \"columns\": [\n845\t          \"injection_rate_p\",\n846\t          \"delta_tpr_standard\",\n847\t          \"delta_tpr_cross_subject\",\n848\t          \"reviewer_spin\"\n849\t        ],\n850\t        \"rows\": [\n851\t          [\n852\t            0.3,\n853\t            0.222,\n854\t            0.18,\n855\t            \"Below useful detection\"\n856\t          ],\n857\t          [\n858\t            0.5,\n859\t            0.508,\n860\t            0.36,\n861\t            \"Only works at 50%+ exploitation\"\n862\t          ],\n863\t          [\n864\t            0.7,\n865\t            0.664,\n866\t            0.52,\n867\t            \"High exploitation = easy detection\"\n868\t          ],\n869\t          [\n870\t            1.0,\n871\t            0.674,\n872\t            0.62,\n873\t            \"Saturates — ceiling effect\"\n874\t          ]\n875\t        ]\n876\t      },\n877\t      {\n878\t        \"name\": \"Table 18: Matching Strategy Comparison\",\n879\t        \"columns\": [\n880\t          \"matching\",\n881\t          \"fpr_baseline\",\n882\t          \"fpr_at_p10\",\n883\t          \"delta_fpr\",\n884\t          \"verdict\"\n885\t        ],\n886\t        \"rows\": [\n887\t          [\n888\t            \"standard_MH\",\n889\t            0.298,\n890\t            0.616,\n891\t            \"+0.318\",\n892\t            \"FPR inflates (contaminated matching var)\"\n893\t          ],\n894\t          [\n895\t            \"external_5subj\",\n896\t            0.314,\n897\t            0.314,\n898\t            0.0,\n899\t            \"FPR stable (clean matching var)\"\n900\t          ],\n901\t          [\n902\t            \"cross_subject\",\n903\t            0.33,\n904\t            0.33,\n905\t            0.0,\n906\t            \"FPR stable (recommended)\"\n907\t          ]\n908\t        ]\n909\t      }\n910\t    ],\n911\t    \"figures\": [],\n912\t    \"analysis\": \"Semi-synthetic validation is the strongest methodological contribution even under worst tier. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is genuinely novel and useful. Under reframe, the semi-synthetic framework is repositioned as \\\"calibration infrastructure for item sensitivity analysis\\\" — it characterizes how much differential signal is needed before DIF detects it, without claiming this maps to real contamination. The dose-response curve becomes \\\"sensitivity calibration\\\" rather than \\\"contamination detection power.\\\" This experiment survives the reframe relatively intact.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, semi-synthetic results (already observed) are technically sound but dismissed on ecological validity grounds. ΔTPR=0.508 at p=0.5 shows DIF detects injected contamination — but reviewers argue the injection mechanism (flip incorrect→correct with probability p) doesn't model real contamination. Real contamination involves: (1) partial memorization (model recalls fragments, not full answers), (2) shallow pattern matching (model exploits surface features), (3) chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises. None of these produce the clean incorrect→correct flipping that synthetic injection assumes. The dose-response curve (ΔTPR increases with p) is called \\\"trivially expected — of course flipping more answers makes items behave more differently.\\\" The cross- subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is acknowledged as methodologically sound, but reviewers argue \\\"you've built a valid framework for detecting something that doesn't exist in the real world in this form.\\\" The gap between synthetic ΔTPR=0.508 and real enrichment=0.96 is the fatal disconnect: \\\"Your method works perfectly on synthetic contamination and not at all on real data — this means your synthetic model is wrong, not that your ground truth is wrong.\\\"\\n\"\n913\t  }\n914\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4252ca06-67da-4be4-a2cb-c5a7e1cf56da","uuid":"24cba543-cf46-4cb5-a094-cb93a326404e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:53:59.024Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply MH DIF to METABENCH MMLU 5,219×12,508 matrix. Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity analysis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n      \"temporal_enrichment\": 0.96,\n      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n      \"reviewer_interpretation\": \"capability_gain_detector\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary Under Worst Interpretation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"cohort_definition\",\n          \"c_pct\",\n          \"enrichment_vs_li\",\n          \"reviewer_counterargument\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"temporal_2023_vs_2024\",\n            31.3,\n            0.96,\n            \"Capability gain across generations\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ability_quintile\",\n            2.9,\n            1.19,\n            \"Minimal DIF = items function consistently across ability levels (expected)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"within_family_llama2v3\",\n            61.3,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"Same family, same data pipeline → pure capability gain\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_split_sanity\",\n            0.18,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"Confirms real signal, but signal = capability\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Domain Correlation Detail\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"value\",\n          \"reviewer_spin\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"spearman_rho_dif_vs_accuracy_gain\",\n            0.307,\n            \"DIF tracks capability improvement\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"p_value\",\n            0.02,\n            \"Significant correlation confirms confound\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"variance_explained\",\n            \"9.4%\",\n            \"Remainder is noise, not contamination signal\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The plan_001 results are empirically sound but narratively fatal under worst tier. The reframe salvages them: 31.3% \\\"instability\\\" becomes \\\"31% of items are capability- sensitive — they differentiate model generations.\\\" ρ=0.307 becomes evidence that DIF identifies items where genuine capability improvement concentrates. Enrichment=0.96 is reframed as \\\"DIF and contamination labels measure orthogonal constructs — DIF captures differential item functioning, not data leakage.\\\" The within-family result becomes the strongest evidence FOR the reframe: even within Llama family, DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest at the item level.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the actual results hold (these are already observed) but their interpretation collapses. ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) correlating DIF flags with domain-level accuracy improvement is seized by reviewers as causal evidence: items flagged by DIF are simply items where models improved. Enrichment=0.96 against Li et al. labels confirms zero relationship to contamination — reviewers read this as \\\"DIF doesn't detect contamination at all.\\\" The 61.3% within-family Llama-2-vs-3 C% is devastating: within the same family (same training pipeline, likely same data), DIF flags 61% of items, which reviewers attribute entirely to capability gain between generations. The sanity check (0.18%) proves DIF detects *something* real, but reviewers argue that \\\"something\\\" is capability improvement, not benchmark pathology. The meta-critique about Li et al. labels is dismissed: \\\"You haven't provided ANY ground truth showing DIF detects contamination — enrichment=0.96 is your only validation, and it failed.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL training. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477) and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N≈13) cohorts. Compare DIF vs Min-K%/ConStat per-model degradation. Power analysis for partial exploitation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_reasoning_rl_models\": 13,\n      \"dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.82,\n      \"dif_flag_overlap_sft_vs_reasoning_rl\": 0.71,\n      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.63,\n      \"mink_auroc_alignment_rl\": 0.58,\n      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.44,\n      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.67,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.51,\n      \"dif_tpr_with_rl_in_cohort\": 0.65,\n      \"dif_tpr_without_rl_in_cohort\": 0.68,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_overlap\": true,\n      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.23,\n      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.34,\n      \"partial_exploitation_detection_boundary\": 0.55,\n      \"reviewer_score\": 4.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: RL Robustness Comparison (Underpowered)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"sft_auroc\",\n          \"alignment_rl_auroc\",\n          \"reasoning_rl_auroc\",\n          \"95% CI width\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF_MH\",\n            0.69,\n            0.66,\n            0.65,\n            \"±0.14\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Min-K%\",\n            0.63,\n            0.58,\n            0.44,\n            \"±0.18\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat\",\n            0.67,\n            0.61,\n            0.51,\n            \"±0.16\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Statistical Tests (All Non-Significant)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"comparison\",\n          \"test\",\n          \"statistic\",\n          \"p_value\",\n          \"conclusion\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF_vs_ConStat_RL\",\n            \"DeLong\",\n            \"z=1.19\",\n            0.23,\n            \"Cannot reject H0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DIF_retention_equivalence\",\n            \"TOST\",\n            \"t=0.96\",\n            0.34,\n            \"Cannot claim equivalence\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"flag_overlap_SFT_vs_RL\",\n            \"Bootstrap Jaccard\",\n            0.71,\n            \"CI [0.54, 0.88]\",\n            \"Wide CI, inconclusive\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Experiment is fatally underpowered. N=13 reasoning-RL models cannot support any statistical claim. Under worst tier, this becomes ammunition: \\\"authors claim RL robustness but provide no evidence.\\\" Reframe option: drop RL robustness as a claim entirely, acknowledge in limitations, reposition the alignment-RL (N=477) result as \\\"DIF is stable across training objective variations\\\" without claiming reasoning-RL coverage. Under the capability-archaeology reframe, RL robustness is less central anyway — the question becomes \\\"do capability-authentic items remain stable across training paradigms?\\\" which is interesting but not load-bearing.\\n\\n\\nWith only N=13 reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical significance. DIF flag overlap between SFT and RL-containing cohorts is 71%, which reviewers dismiss as \\\"you can't distinguish robustness from noise with N=13.\\\" The DeLong test p=0.23 means we cannot claim DIF outperforms ConStat. TOST equivalence test fails (p=0.34 > 0.05) — we can't even claim equivalence. Min-K% drops to 0.44 AUROC on reasoning-RL models, but reviewers note \\\"you're comparing DIF (group-level, N=477 cohort) against per-model detectors on 13 models — apples to oranges.\\\" The partial exploitation analysis shows detection boundary at p=0.55, meaning DIF misses contamination below 55% exploitation rate — reviewers call this \\\"DIF only detects extreme contamination, which is also detectable by simpler methods.\\\" The entire experiment is underpowered and reviewers recommend \\\"defer until more GRPO/PPO models exist on public leaderboards.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation evaluation on 200+ MMLU items. Generate semantic reformulations, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_reformulated\": 220,\n      \"n_models_tested\": 15,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_non_rl\": 0.41,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_rl\": 0.12,\n      \"constat_fdr_controlled_rate\": 0.28,\n      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap\": 0.18,\n      \"paraphrase_quality_score\": 0.73,\n      \"reformulation_difficulty_shift_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"paraphrase quality uncontrolled\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: ConStat vs DIF Detection Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"detection_target\",\n          \"rate_non_rl\",\n          \"rate_rl\",\n          \"per_model\",\n          \"requires_cohort\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ConStat\",\n            \"memorization\",\n            0.41,\n            0.12,\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DIF_MH\",\n            \"differential_functioning\",\n            0.313,\n            \"N/A (group-level)\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"overlap\",\n            \"both_flag\",\n            0.18,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"-\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ConStat comparison backfires under worst tier. It provides a foil that makes DIF look less like a contamination detector. Under reframe, this becomes irrelevant — DIF is repositioned as capability-item identifier, not competing with ConStat on contamination detection. Drop from reframed paper or move to appendix as \\\"related method comparison showing orthogonal signals.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, ConStat shows moderate detection on non-RL models (41%) — notably higher than DIF's enrichment=0.96 against contamination labels. Reviewers argue \\\"ConStat at least detects something correlated with memorization; DIF detects capability improvement.\\\" The 18% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags confirms they measure different constructs, but reviewers spin this negatively: \\\"DIF is orthogonal to contamination detection.\\\" The RL degradation (41%→12%) is expected but reviewers note \\\"at least ConStat provides per-model detection; DIF requires cohort structure.\\\" Paraphrase quality (0.73) is attacked: \\\"reformulations may introduce difficulty shifts (mean Δ=0.08), confounding detection.\\\" The entire comparison undermines DIF rather than supporting it — ConStat looks like the more direct contamination tool, and DIF looks like a capability change detector with no contamination detection ability.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance evaluation on 10-15 open-weight models. 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection for contamination.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models\": 12,\n      \"n_items_tested\": 500,\n      \"dvd_bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.23,\n      \"dvd_variance_auc\": 0.58,\n      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap\": 0.14,\n      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"high (results vary 15-30% across T=0.7 vs T=1.0)\",\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 65,\n      \"generation_cost_barrier\": \"high\",\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"expensive and noisy\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: DVD Results Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"value\",\n          \"stability\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bimodal_detection_rate\",\n            0.23,\n            \"±0.09 across temperatures\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"variance_auc\",\n            0.58,\n            \"±0.07\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"dif_overlap\",\n            0.14,\n            \"stable\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"cost_per_model\",\n            \"5.4 GPU-hrs\",\n            \"fixed\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DVD is the weakest experiment under worst tier. Low AUC, high cost, temperature sensitivity, minimal DIF overlap. Under reframe, DVD is irrelevant — drop entirely. The capability-archaeology framing doesn't need generation variance.\\n\\n\\nDVD shows weak detection (AUC=0.58, barely above chance) on only 12 models. The bimodal detection rate of 23% is unstable across temperature settings (15-30% variation), making the signal unreliable. Overlap with DIF is minimal (14%), confirming both methods detect different phenomena. Under worst tier, reviewers dismiss DVD as \\\"noisy, expensive, and only applicable to open-weight models.\\\" The 65 GPU-hour cost for testing 12 models × 500 items is criticized as impractical. More importantly, DVD's failure doesn't help DIF's case — reviewers note \\\"neither DVD nor DIF convincingly detects contamination; DIF detects capability change, DVD detects generation instability, neither detects what we actually care about.\\\" The experiment consumes resources without strengthening any claim.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Majority voting and weighted fusion. Cross-validate fusion weights.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ensemble_2of3_precision\": 0.31,\n      \"ensemble_2of3_recall\": 0.15,\n      \"ensemble_weighted_auc\": 0.62,\n      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.14,\n      \"uniform_weight_auc\": 0.59,\n      \"optimized_weight_auc\": 0.64,\n      \"cv_overfit_gap\": 0.08,\n      \"dif_weight_in_optimized\": 0.22,\n      \"constat_weight_in_optimized\": 0.51,\n      \"dvd_weight_in_optimized\": 0.27,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"ensemble of weak signals is still weak\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Ensemble Performance (Worst Tier)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"strategy\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"recall\",\n          \"auc\",\n          \"fpr\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF_only\",\n            0.29,\n            0.31,\n            0.54,\n            0.31\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat_only\",\n            0.38,\n            0.41,\n            0.61,\n            0.22\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DVD_only\",\n            0.24,\n            0.23,\n            0.58,\n            0.28\n          ],\n          [\n            \"majority_2of3\",\n            0.31,\n            0.15,\n            0.55,\n            0.14\n          ],\n          [\n            \"weighted_fusion_cv\",\n            0.35,\n            0.32,\n            0.64,\n            0.19\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble experiment is counterproductive under worst tier. It demonstrates DIF's weakness relative to ConStat for contamination detection. Under reframe, the entire plan_003 sequence should be dropped — the capability-archaeology paper doesn't need contamination detection ensemble. Save 150 GPU-hours.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, ensemble fusion fails to produce meaningful improvement. The 2-of-3 majority voting achieves only 15% recall because DIF, ConStat, and DVD flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14-0.18). Weighted fusion (AUC=0.64) barely beats uniform (0.59), and cross-validation reveals 0.08 overfit gap, suggesting the optimized weights don't generalize. Critically, DIF receives the lowest weight (0.22) in the optimized ensemble — reviewers read this as \\\"even the ensemble agrees DIF contributes least to contamination detection.\\\" The ensemble FPR of 0.14 technically meets the <0.10 target... no, it doesn't (0.14 > 0.10). Reviewers conclude: \\\"three individually weak contamination signals don't combine into a strong one. The fundamental problem — lack of ground truth — isn't solved by fusion.\\\" The entire plan_003 sequence (a+b+c) consumed 150 GPU-hours for a negative meta-result.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models). Semi-synthetic validation. GSM1K contamination gap correlation as external validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gsm8k_temporal_c_pct\": 18.9,\n      \"gsm8k_semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n      \"gsm1k_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n      \"gsm1k_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n      \"gsm1k_partial_r\": -0.385,\n      \"gsm1k_n_models\": 33,\n      \"gsm1k_bootstrap_ci_r\": [\n        -0.71,\n        -0.12\n      ],\n      \"gsm1k_permutation_p\": 0.014,\n      \"cook_d_max\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n      \"leave_one_out_min_r\": -0.374,\n      \"reviewer_reinterpretation\": \"anti-correlation confirms DIF=capability, not contamination\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: GSM1K Correlation Under Worst-Case Scrutiny\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"point_estimate\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"reviewer_counterargument\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"pearson_r\",\n            -0.478,\n            \"[-0.71, -0.12]\",\n            \"CI includes near-zero; N=33 too small\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"partial_r\",\n            -0.385,\n            \"[-0.64, -0.04]\",\n            \"Controlling accuracy weakens signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"permutation_p\",\n            0.014,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"Multiple testing not corrected\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"leave_one_out_min\",\n            -0.374,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"One model removal cuts 22% of effect\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Cross-Benchmark C% Instability\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"benchmark\",\n          \"temporal_c_pct\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            31.3,\n            \"High instability or high capability sensitivity\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            18.9,\n            \"Lower instability — benchmark-dependent\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"delta\",\n            12.4,\n            \"No universal 'instability rate' — DIF is benchmark-specific\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The GSM1K correlation is the pivotal result. Under original framing, r=−0.478 is devastating (DIF anti-correlates with contamination). Under reframe, it becomes the HEADLINE: \\\"DIF identifies capability-authentic items that contamination cannot replicate.\\\" The wide CI (N=33) remains a vulnerability. The reframed paper should lead with this finding and frame it as: \\\"Contaminated models show inflated scores on non-DIF items (shallow memorization) but cannot maintain performance on DIF items (genuine capability discriminators).\\\" ΔTPR=0.818 is repositioned as \\\"calibration infrastructure\\\" rather than \\\"detection proof.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, the r=−0.478 result — the strongest cross-benchmark finding — is turned against the original narrative. Reviewers argue: \\\"DIF items anti-correlate with contamination gap. Models that are MORE contaminated (high GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Phi, Gemma) perform WORSE on DIF items. This proves DIF identifies items where genuine capability matters — contaminated models can't fake their way through these items. DIF is a capability detector, not a contamination detector.\\\" The bootstrap CI [-0.71, -0.12] is wide (N=33), and reviewers note \\\"this could be as weak as r=-0.12, barely meaningful.\\\" The ΔTPR=0.818 on semi-synthetic injection is dismissed: \\\"synthetic contamination (flipping incorrect→correct deterministically) doesn't resemble real contamination (partial memorization, shallow pattern matching). High ΔTPR on synthetic data doesn't validate real-world detection.\\\" The 18.9% temporal C% on GSM8K (vs 31.3% on MMLU) is spun as \\\"DIF flags vary wildly across benchmarks — no stable characterization.\\\" Deepseek-math-7b-rl as Cook's D outlier raises concerns about robustness of the correlation despite leave-one-out showing min |r|=0.374.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT on MMLU (general factor + 57 subject factors). Anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration for replacement items.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bifactor_convergence\": false,\n      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.11,\n      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.72,\n      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.68,\n      \"n_subjects_57\": 57,\n      \"n_items_12508\": 12508,\n      \"n_models_5219\": 5219,\n      \"anchor_items_selected\": 0,\n      \"equating_rho\": \"N/A (model failed to converge)\",\n      \"computation_time_hours\": 48,\n      \"memory_peak_gb\": 180,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"57-factor bifactor on 12K items is computationally intractable\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Bifactor IRT Fit Diagnostics\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"value\",\n          \"threshold\",\n          \"verdict\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"RMSEA\",\n            0.11,\n            \"<0.08\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CFI\",\n            0.72,\n            \">0.90\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"TLI\",\n            0.68,\n            \">0.90\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SRMR\",\n            0.09,\n            \"<0.08\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"convergence\",\n            \"No (48h timeout)\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"n_parameters\",\n            \"~25,000\",\n            \"<5,000 practical\",\n            \"FAIL\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor failure is expected and, under worst tier, becomes evidence that MMLU is fundamentally unsuitable for IRT-based psychometric analysis. Reviewers cite this as proof that \\\"applying unidimensional psychometric methods to a 57-subject multidimensional benchmark is methodologically invalid.\\\" Under reframe, this is acknowledged honestly in limitations: \\\"MMLU's multidimensional structure exceeds current IRT modeling capacity; our within-subject analysis (plan_014) partially addresses this but doesn't eliminate the concern.\\\" Drop the equating pipeline entirely.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, bifactor IRT fails catastrophically. The 57-subject structure of MMLU means a bifactor model requires estimating 1 general + 57 specific factors — a 58-dimensional latent space with ~25,000 item parameters. This exceeds practical computational limits (180GB RAM, 48 hours without convergence). Even if forced to converge with simplifications (e.g., constraining factor loadings), fit indices are poor: RMSEA=0.11 (>0.08 threshold), CFI=0.72 (<0.90 threshold), TLI=0.68 (<0.90). The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is confirmed as violated, but the alternative (bifactor) is intractable. Reviewers argue: \\\"You've demonstrated that the psychometric foundation of your method is inappropriate for MMLU, but provided no working alternative. Multidimensional DIF methods exist (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) but you haven't implemented them.\\\" Anchor item selection fails because the bifactor model didn't converge — no equating is possible. The entire regeneration pipeline (plan_005 → plan_006 → plan_008) collapses at step one.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. LLM generation with construct constraints, student simulation calibration, difficulty correlation validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_generated\": 300,\n      \"n_subjects_covered\": 15,\n      \"difficulty_correlation_prompt\": 0.18,\n      \"difficulty_correlation_student_sim\": 0.34,\n      \"cognitive_level_match_rate\": 0.45,\n      \"item_quality_human_eval\": 0.61,\n      \"construct_alignment_score\": 0.53,\n      \"holdout_panel_rho\": 0.42,\n      \"gpu_hours_used\": 180,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"difficulty calibration too weak for operational use\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Item Generation Calibration Failure\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"calibration_method\",\n          \"difficulty_rho\",\n          \"claimed_in_literature\",\n          \"achieved\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"prompt_targeting\",\n            0.18,\n            \"0.10-0.45\",\n            \"Low end\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"student_simulation\",\n            0.34,\n            \"0.75-0.82\",\n            \"Far below\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"holdout_validation\",\n            0.42,\n            \">0.90 needed\",\n            \"Insufficient\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Item generation is the most expensive failure (180 GPU-hrs). Under worst tier, it actively undermines the paper — reviewers use it to argue \\\"you can't replace DIF items, and you've shown DIF items don't affect rankings (ρ=0.997), so what's the practical value?\\\" Under reframe, this experiment should not be run at all. The capability-archaeology paper frames DIF items as valuable signal carriers, not problems to be replaced. Save the 200 GPU-hour budget.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, item generation produces mediocre results. Prompt-based difficulty targeting yields ρ=0.18 (consistent with literature range 0.10-0.45 from arXiv 2602.06631 — on the low end). Student simulation calibration improves to ρ=0.34 but still far below the r=0.75-0.82 claimed in arXiv 2601.09953 — the discrepancy arises because student simulation was validated on simple math items, not MMLU's diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45%) means over half of replacement items test different cognitive processes than originals. Construct alignment (53%) is poor — reviewers argue \\\"replacing items with construct-misaligned substitutes invalidates the benchmark more than the DIF items did.\\\" The holdout panel validation (ρ=0.42) shows replacement items don't preserve model rankings. Reviewers conclude: \\\"The cure is worse than the disease — DIF-flagged items at least produce stable rankings (ρ=0.997); replacements introduce genuine measurement error.\\\" 180 GPU-hours consumed for a negative result. Under reframe, this experiment is dropped entirely — the capability-archaeology paper doesn't need item replacement.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006). Simulate 4 successive administrations. Show power improvement from evidence accumulation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"single_admin_power_d10\": 0.38,\n      \"cumulative_2_admin_power_d10\": 0.49,\n      \"cumulative_3_admin_power_d10\": 0.58,\n      \"cumulative_4_admin_power_d10\": 0.64,\n      \"single_admin_fpr\": 0.05,\n      \"cumulative_4_admin_fpr\": 0.11,\n      \"fpr_inflation_per_admin\": 0.02,\n      \"convergence_rate\": 0.92,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"cumulative FPR inflation makes this impractical\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF Power Trajectory\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"n_administrations\",\n          \"tpr_d10\",\n          \"tpr_d14\",\n          \"fpr\",\n          \"net_power_gain\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.38,\n            0.68,\n            0.05,\n            \"baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            0.49,\n            0.78,\n            0.07,\n            \"+0.11/+0.10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            0.58,\n            0.84,\n            0.09,\n            \"+0.09/+0.06\"\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            0.64,\n            0.87,\n            0.11,\n            \"+0.06/+0.03\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian is theoretically elegant but practically undermined by FPR inflation. Under worst tier, it's a distraction — reviewers see diminishing returns and growing false positives. Under reframe, it has modest value as \\\"longitudinal capability tracking infrastructure\\\" but is not central. Recommend moving to appendix or future work.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows power improvement (0.38→0.64 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) but fails to reach the 0.70 target from the plan description. More critically, FPR inflates from 0.05 to 0.11 across 4 administrations — each administration's prior carries forward false positive evidence. Reviewers argue: \\\"With baseline FPR already at 31% in real data (plan_001), cumulative approaches will amplify false positives. You're accumulating evidence that items changed function — but we've established this is capability improvement, not contamination. You're building a more powerful detector of the wrong thing.\\\" The simulation uses idealized conditions (known item parameters, controlled drift) that don't match real MMLU conditions. Under reframe, this becomes interesting — \\\"cumulative tracking of capability-sensitive items across model generations\\\" — but the FPR inflation concern remains.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement (5%-50%). Validity metrics, theoretical bounds, empirical validation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rho_at_5pct_replacement\": 0.998,\n      \"rho_at_10pct_replacement\": 0.996,\n      \"rho_at_20pct_replacement\": 0.991,\n      \"rho_at_50pct_replacement\": 0.972,\n      \"reliability_alpha_original\": 0.94,\n      \"reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced\": 0.88,\n      \"construct_coverage_original\": 57,\n      \"construct_coverage_50pct_replaced\": 52,\n      \"theoretical_bound_rho_min\": 0.95,\n      \"replacement_rate_at_bound\": 0.38,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"confirms DIF removal doesn't matter\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: Graceful Degradation Curves\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"replacement_pct\",\n          \"rank_rho\",\n          \"reliability_alpha\",\n          \"subjects_covered\",\n          \"verdict\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            1.0,\n            0.94,\n            57,\n            \"baseline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            5,\n            0.998,\n            0.93,\n            57,\n            \"negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            10,\n            0.996,\n            0.93,\n            57,\n            \"negligible\"\n          ],\n          [\n            20,\n            0.991,\n            0.91,\n            56,\n            \"minor\"\n          ],\n          [\n            31,\n            0.987,\n            0.9,\n            55,\n            \"DIF removal point\"\n          ],\n          [\n            50,\n            0.972,\n            0.88,\n            52,\n            \"still acceptable\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation is self-defeating under worst tier AND original framing. It proves removing DIF items doesn't matter, undermining significance. Under reframe, this becomes: \\\"MMLU is 3× larger than needed for stable rankings — DIF identifies the informative core (the items that DO contribute discriminative signal).\\\" The ρ=0.972 at 50% replacement becomes \\\"you could cut MMLU in half and preserve rankings — DIF tells you which half to keep.\\\" This is actually a strong reframe, turning the worst finding into a measurement efficiency contribution.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, graceful degradation analysis confirms the ρ=0.997 finding from plan_017 — removing/replacing up to 50% of items barely affects rankings (ρ=0.972). Reviewers weaponize this: \\\"Even replacing HALF the benchmark changes rankings by less than 3%. This proves MMLU is massively redundant, and DIF-flagged items are redundant too. Why should anyone care about 31% item instability if it doesn't affect rankings?\\\" Reliability drops from 0.94 to 0.88 at 50% replacement, but remains above acceptable thresholds (0.70). Construct coverage loses 5 of 57 subjects at 50% replacement — reviewers note \\\"acceptable.\\\" The theoretical bound (ρ≥0.95 up to 38% replacement) perfectly matches the DIF flagging rate (31.3%), which reviewers read as \\\"you can safely ignore everything DIF flags.\\\" This experiment depends on plan_005/006 (which fail), so the \\\"replacement\\\" is simulated via random item substitution rather than actual generated items — further reducing credibility.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical cohort DIF. Continuous covariate (release date). Compare detection power vs categorical MH.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ift_detection_rate\": 0.34,\n      \"ift_vs_mh_overlap\": 0.78,\n      \"ift_unique_flags\": 0.06,\n      \"mh_unique_flags\": 0.04,\n      \"ift_uses_full_sample\": true,\n      \"ift_avoids_cohort_splits\": true,\n      \"ift_computational_cost_ratio\": 12.0,\n      \"ift_release_date_coefficient_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"ift_release_date_coefficient_std\": 0.15,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"confirms temporal trend = capability gain\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 14: IFT vs MH Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"flagging_rate\",\n          \"unique_flags\",\n          \"cost_ratio\",\n          \"temporal_pattern\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MH_categorical\",\n            0.313,\n            0.04,\n            1.0,\n            \"Discrete cohort split\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"IFT_continuous\",\n            0.34,\n            0.06,\n            12.0,\n            \"Smooth positive trend\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"overlap\",\n            0.78,\n            \"-\",\n            \"-\",\n            \"Same underlying signal\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides marginal methodological value but under worst tier confirms the capability-improvement interpretation via smooth temporal trends. Under reframe, the smooth trend is actually interesting: \\\"capability-authentic items show predictable sensitivity to model generation — enabling prospective identification of items that will become less discriminative.\\\" Recommend as supplementary/appendix rather than main paper contribution.\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 34% of items as having significant temporal DIF — nearly identical to MH's 31.3%. The 78% overlap confirms both methods detect the same phenomenon. The IFT release-date coefficient (mean=0.08, SD=0.15) is positive: items become easier over time, consistent with capability improvement. Reviewers read the continuous coefficient as: \\\"DIF is a smooth function of time, not a discrete shift — this is exactly what capability improvement looks like. If contamination caused DIF, we'd expect discrete jumps when specific training data became available.\\\" The 12× computational cost over MH makes IFT impractical at scale. Reviewers conclude: \\\"IFT confirms MH findings but adds computational cost and the interpretation that item changes are gradual (capability) not abrupt (contamination).\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only homogeneous subpopulation. IRT fit diagnostics. Compare FPR/TPR vs full-population DIF from plan_001.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_decoder_only\": 3400,\n      \"decoder_only_c_pct\": 33.1,\n      \"decoder_only_irt_rmsea\": 0.092,\n      \"decoder_only_irt_cfi\": 0.81,\n      \"decoder_only_irt_tli\": 0.78,\n      \"decoder_only_irt_srmr\": 0.073,\n      \"full_population_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"delta_c_pct\": 1.8,\n      \"fpr_decoder_only\": 0.33,\n      \"fpr_full_population\": 0.318,\n      \"tpr_delta_p05\": -0.02,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"architecture homogeneity doesn't fix the problem\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 15: Architecture Stratification Results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"population\",\n          \"n_models\",\n          \"c_pct\",\n          \"rmsea\",\n          \"cfi\",\n          \"tli\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"full_population\",\n            5219,\n            31.3,\n            \"N/A (not computed)\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"N/A\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"decoder_only\",\n            3400,\n            33.1,\n            0.092,\n            0.81,\n            0.78\n          ],\n          [\n            \"delta\",\n            \"-1819\",\n            \"+1.8\",\n            \"FAIL (>0.08)\",\n            \"FAIL (<0.90)\",\n            \"FAIL (<0.90)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 16: Detection Power Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"population\",\n          \"fpr_baseline\",\n          \"tpr_p05\",\n          \"tpr_p10\",\n          \"enrichment\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"full_population\",\n            0.318,\n            0.834,\n            1.0,\n            0.96\n          ],\n          [\n            \"decoder_only\",\n            0.33,\n            0.82,\n            0.99,\n            0.95\n          ],\n          [\n            \"delta\",\n            \"+0.012\",\n            \"-0.014\",\n            \"-0.01\",\n            \"-0.01\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification is the critical missing experiment identified by worst-case Dream, and it produces the predicted negative result. Under worst tier, this closes the last escape route: DIF flagging rates cannot be reduced by controlling for architecture. The IRT misfit is structural (MMLU dimensionality) not population-driven. Under reframe, this becomes neutral: \\\"DIF item sensitivity is robust to population composition — capability-authentic items are identified regardless of architecture mix.\\\" The IRT fit concern is acknowledged honestly but framed as \\\"conservative: poor IRT fit means DIF may UNDERESTIMATE the true proportion of capability-sensitive items.\\\"\\n\\n\\nUnder worst tier (explicitly specified in tier definition), architecture stratification fails to improve results. Decoder-only C% = 33.1% (vs 31.3% full population) — no meaningful reduction, slightly HIGHER. IRT fit remains poor: RMSEA=0.092 (>0.08), CFI=0.81 (<0.90), TLI=0.78 (<0.90). Removing encoder-decoder and hybrid architectures doesn't address the fundamental unidimensionality problem — MMLU's 57-subject structure violates unidimensional IRT regardless of architecture homogeneity. The C% increase from 31.3%→33.1% is within noise but reviewers spin it: \\\"architecture stratification made things WORSE — DIF flags more items in a homogeneous population, confirming that flags reflect capability variation, not architecture confounding.\\\" FPR and TPR are essentially unchanged (ΔFPR=+0.01, ΔTPR=−0.02). Reviewers familiar with psychometrics argue: \\\"RMSEA=0.092 on decoder-only models proves the IRT model is misspecified for MMLU. Your entire DIF analysis rests on an inappropriate measurement model. This is not fixable by subsetting — it requires multidimensional IRT, which you haven't implemented.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix. Measure DIF TPR/FPR against controlled ground truth. Vary injection parameters.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.222,\n      \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n      \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.664,\n      \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n      \"external_matching_fpr\": 0.314,\n      \"within_matching_fpr_inflation\": 0.186,\n      \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n      \"cross_subject_delta_tpr_p05_avg\": 0.36,\n      \"dose_response_present\": true,\n      \"reviewer_critique\": \"synthetic injection mechanism unrealistic\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 17: Semi-Synthetic Results Under Worst Interpretation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"injection_rate_p\",\n          \"delta_tpr_standard\",\n          \"delta_tpr_cross_subject\",\n          \"reviewer_spin\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0.3,\n            0.222,\n            0.18,\n            \"Below useful detection\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.5,\n            0.508,\n            0.36,\n            \"Only works at 50%+ exploitation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            0.7,\n            0.664,\n            0.52,\n            \"High exploitation = easy detection\"\n          ],\n          [\n            1.0,\n            0.674,\n            0.62,\n            \"Saturates — ceiling effect\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 18: Matching Strategy Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"matching\",\n          \"fpr_baseline\",\n          \"fpr_at_p10\",\n          \"delta_fpr\",\n          \"verdict\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"standard_MH\",\n            0.298,\n            0.616,\n            \"+0.318\",\n            \"FPR inflates (contaminated matching var)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"external_5subj\",\n            0.314,\n            0.314,\n            0.0,\n            \"FPR stable (clean matching var)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"cross_subject\",\n            0.33,\n            0.33,\n            0.0,\n            \"FPR stable (recommended)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Semi-synthetic validation is the strongest methodological contribution even under worst tier. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is genuinely novel and useful. Under reframe, the semi-synthetic framework is repositioned as \\\"calibration infrastructure for item sensitivity analysis\\\" — it characterizes how much differential signal is needed before DIF detects it, without claiming this maps to real contamination. The dose-response curve becomes \\\"sensitivity calibration\\\" rather than \\\"contamination detection power.\\\" This experiment survives the reframe relatively intact.\\n\\nUnder worst tier, semi-synthetic results (already observed) are technically sound but dismissed on ecological validity grounds. ΔTPR=0.508 at p=0.5 shows DIF detects injected contamination — but reviewers argue the injection mechanism (flip incorrect→correct with probability p) doesn't model real contamination. Real contamination involves: (1) partial memorization (model recalls fragments, not full answers), (2) shallow pattern matching (model exploits surface features), (3) chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises. None of these produce the clean incorrect→correct flipping that synthetic injection assumes. The dose-response curve (ΔTPR increases with p) is called \\\"trivially expected — of course flipping more answers makes items behave more differently.\\\" The cross- subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000) is acknowledged as methodologically sound, but reviewers argue \\\"you've built a valid framework for detecting something that doesn't exist in the real world in this form.\\\" The gap between synthetic ΔTPR=0.508 and real enrichment=0.96 is the fatal disconnect: \\\"Your method works perfectly on synthetic contamination and not at all on real data — this means your synthetic model is wrong, not that your ground truth is wrong.\\\"\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":914,"startLine":1,"totalLines":914}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TLGbLZpQLtuvUWpTCvNQoB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the experiment data and the paper framing. Let me also check if the output directory exists.\n\nThe narrative guidance says this is the \"worst\" tier — the core narrative collapses. Reviewers see DIF as a capability improvement detector, not a benchmark integrity tool. The reframe is to pivot to \"DIF as capability archaeology / measurement efficiency\" paper.\n\nLet me write a complete LaTeX paper following the narrative guidance for the \"worst\" tier. The key reframing:\n- 38% instability = 38% of items are capability-sensitive\n\n\n- The correlation coefficient of 0.307 is the critical evidence that DIF genuinely captures capability differences\n- GSM1K's negative correlation becomes the main positive result, showing DIF successfully filters out contaminated items\n- The near-perfect correlation suggests benchmarks are substantially oversized\n- Semi-synthetic injection serves as calibration infrastructure\n- I'll acknowledge the bifactor model limitations directly\n- Focus on the core DIF findings and drop the ensemble, DVD, and item generation components as tangential\n- Title the paper around identifying capability-authentic items through differential item functioning\n\nNow I'm structuring the paper with abstract, introduction, and background sections.\n\nFor the experiments, I need to cover all twelve studies from the dataset. Looking at the full list, I can see which ones support the main narrative about DIF identifying genuine capability versus contamination, and which ones represent negative or exploratory results that I should present honestly rather than hide.\n\nThe strongest findings center on real MMLU data showing DIF's effectiveness, the GSM8K anti-correlation as a key headline result, evidence that benchmarks are redundant and we could measure more efficiently, and the semi-synthetic validation framework proving the approach works. The weaker experiments—robustness validation being underpowered, ConStat showing orthogonal rather than overlapping signals, DVD being both weak and computationally expensive, ensemble methods failing, bifactor IRT not working, item generation calibration issues, Bayesian cumulative DIF showing false positive rate inflation, and architecture stratification providing no benefit—all get honest treatment as negative or null results rather than being buried.\n\nItem-focused decision trees confirm the capability interpretation holds up, and now I'm ready to write the paper with comprehensive coverage while keeping it at an appropriate level of rigor.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3326,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14358,"cache_read_input_tokens":23299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14358},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4252ca06-67da-4be4-a2cb-c5a7e1cf56da","uuid":"774cb004-6b4b-403d-953f-8c87c3ae70d5"}
