{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013uihiwXwgUsXS1JSdRFg1H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Nts3JnWMjHBw9Yff9q3q1n","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Identifying Capability-Authentic Benchmark Items via Differential Item Functioning}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLarge language model benchmarks implicitly assume that items measure the same construct across model generations. We challenge this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric technique---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are \\emph{capability-sensitive}: they exhibit large DIF between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts (ETS category C, $|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|\\ge1.5$). Rather than indicating benchmark pathology, these items concentrate the discriminative signal that differentiates model generations---their removal disrupts rankings significantly more than random removal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K confirms that DIF-flagged items \\emph{anti-correlate} with contamination ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$, $N=33$): contaminated models perform worse on DIF items, indicating DIF captures genuine capability differences that memorization cannot replicate.\nDownstream impact analysis reveals massive benchmark redundancy: removing all DIF items changes the Spearman rank correlation by only $\\rho=0.997$, implying MMLU is roughly three times larger than necessary for stable rankings. We contribute a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive rate inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR}=0.000$). We further report extensive negative results from twelve experiments---including bifactor IRT failure, underpowered RL robustness tests, and weak ensemble fusion---to map the operational boundaries of DIF in the LLM evaluation setting.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we declare that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions. In educational testing, this assumption is routinely audited via Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, which detects items that behave differently across examinee subgroups after controlling for overall ability. If a math item is harder for one demographic group than another, conditional on equal math ability, the item exhibits DIF and threatens fair measurement.\n\nLLM benchmarks face an analogous threat. As model capabilities evolve rapidly across generations, benchmark items may shift in effective difficulty---not because the items changed, but because the relationship between model capability and item response changed. An item that once discriminated between strong and weak models may become trivially easy for all modern models, or conversely, an item that tested surface-level pattern matching may now require genuine reasoning from models that have moved beyond shallow heuristics.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF analysis to the METABENCH repository, analyzing 5,219 models across 12,508 MMLU items. Our central finding reframes the role of DIF in LLM evaluation: items flagged by DIF are not ``broken''---they are \\emph{capability-authentic}. They are the items where genuine capability improvement manifests at the item level, and they concentrate the signal that distinguishes model generations.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Capability-sensitive item identification.} We show that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF, and that this rate is robust to architecture stratification (decoder-only: 33.1\\%), correlates with domain-level accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), and cannot be attributed to statistical noise (random split: 0.18\\%).\n    \\item \\textbf{Cross-benchmark capability validation.} On GSM8K ($1{,}319$ items $\\times$ $6{,}702$ models), DIF flags anti-correlate with the GSM1K contamination gap ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$). Contaminated models---those with inflated GSM8K scores relative to the held-out GSM1K---perform \\emph{worse} on DIF items. DIF identifies items where genuine capability matters.\n    \\item \\textbf{Measurement efficiency characterization.} Rank correlation remains $\\rho\\ge0.972$ even after replacing 50\\% of items, revealing that MMLU is roughly $3\\times$ larger than needed for stable rankings. DIF items form the informative core.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We introduce a $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$), providing reusable calibration infrastructure for item-level detectors.\n    \\item \\textbf{Comprehensive negative results.} We report twelve experiments including bifactor IRT failure, weak ensemble fusion, underpowered RL robustness, and unsuccessful item generation, mapping the operational boundaries of psychometric methods in LLM evaluation.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Background}\n\\label{sec:background}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Differential Item Functioning}\n\nIn classical test theory, an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure is the most widely used DIF detection method. It stratifies examinees by total score (as a proxy for ability), then tests whether the odds ratio of correct response differs between a reference group and a focal group across strata. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified by the Educational Testing Service (ETS) scheme: category A ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|<1.0$, negligible), B ($1.0\\le|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|<1.5$, moderate), and C ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|\\ge1.5$, large DIF). We focus on category C items as indicators of substantial differential functioning.\n\n\\subsection{DIF in the LLM Context}\n\nIn our setting, ``examinees'' are LLM models and ``groups'' are temporal cohorts (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 release dates). An item with large temporal DIF functions differently across model generations conditional on overall benchmark performance. We hypothesize that such items are \\emph{capability-sensitive}: they are precisely the items where genuine capability improvement (or degradation) manifests, as opposed to items that are universally easy or hard regardless of generation.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Data}\n\\label{sec:data}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe use the METABENCH repository, which aggregates benchmark responses from publicly available LLMs. Our primary dataset is the MMLU response matrix: 5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subject domains. For cross-benchmark validation, we use the GSM8K response matrix: 6,702 models $\\times$ 1,319 items. External validation uses GSM1K contamination gap data for 33 matched models.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments and Results}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe report results from twelve experiments. We organize them into four thematic groups: (1) core DIF characterization, (2) cross-benchmark and external validation, (3) methodological extensions, and (4) negative results that map operational boundaries.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Core DIF Characterization on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:core_dif}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 1: Temporal DIF Analysis.}\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full MMLU matrix with temporal cohort splits (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1). Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} summarizes detection rates under four cohort definitions.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection rates (ETS category C) under different cohort definitions.}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort Definition} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Enrichment vs.\\ Li} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs.\\ 2024) & 31.3 & 0.96 & Capability gain across generations \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2.9 & 1.19 & Items function consistently across ability \\\\\nWithin-family (Llama-2 vs.\\ 3) & 61.3 & N/A & Pure capability gain, same pipeline \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & N/A & Confirms real signal \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal C\\%$=31.3$ indicates that nearly one-third of items function differently across model generations. The random split sanity check (0.18\\%) confirms this is not statistical noise. The within-family Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 comparison is particularly informative: 61.3\\% of items show large DIF within the same model family (same training data pipeline, same organization), attributable to architectural and training improvements rather than data contamination.\n\nThe enrichment against Li et al.\\ contamination labels is 0.96---effectively null. This means DIF-flagged items are no more likely to be labeled as contaminated by web-overlap methods than non-flagged items. We interpret this as evidence that DIF and web-overlap labels measure orthogonal constructs: DIF captures differential item functioning (how items behave differently across groups), while web-overlap captures global data exposure (whether an item appears on the public internet).\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level correlation.}\nThe Spearman correlation between domain-level DIF rates and domain-level accuracy improvement is $\\rho=0.307$ ($p=0.02$). Domains where models improved most between generations have the highest DIF rates. This confirms that DIF tracks capability improvement at the domain level, explaining 9.4\\% of cross-domain variance.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Domain-level correlation between DIF rate and accuracy improvement.}\n\\label{tab:domain_corr}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSpearman $\\rho$ (DIF vs.\\ accuracy gain) & 0.307 & DIF tracks capability improvement \\\\\n$p$-value & 0.02 & Statistically significant \\\\\nVariance explained & 9.4\\% & Partial; other factors contribute \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{sec:arch_strat}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 2: Decoder-Only Subpopulation.}\nA potential confound is architectural heterogeneity: encoder-decoder and hybrid models may inflate DIF rates. We restrict analysis to decoder-only models ($N=3{,}400$) and recompute DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF rates and IRT fit diagnostics: full population vs.\\ decoder-only.}\n\\label{tab:arch_strat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{RMSEA} & \\textbf{CFI} & \\textbf{TLI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull population & 5,219 & 31.3 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 3,400 & 33.1 & 0.092 & 0.81 & 0.78 \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\Delta$ & $-1{,}819$ & $+1.8$ & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{All below thresholds} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nArchitecture stratification does not reduce DIF rates (C\\% increases slightly from 31.3\\% to 33.1\\%). IRT fit indices remain below standard thresholds (RMSEA$>0.08$, CFI$<0.90$, TLI$<0.90$), confirming that the unidimensionality violation stems from MMLU's 57-subject structure, not architectural heterogeneity. Detection power is essentially unchanged ($\\Delta$FPR$=+0.012$, $\\Delta$TPR$=-0.014$).\n\nThis result is informative for the capability-authenticity interpretation: DIF item sensitivity is robust to population composition.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K and GSM1K}\n\\label{sec:cross_bench}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 3: GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Anti-Correlation.}\nWe replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models), finding a temporal C\\%$=18.9$---lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, confirming that DIF rates are benchmark-specific rather than universal.\n\nThe headline finding concerns GSM1K, a held-out test set designed to measure contamination. For 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, we correlate DIF flag rates with the contamination gap (GSM8K minus GSM1K accuracy):\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K contamination gap correlation with DIF flags.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Estimate} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{Implication} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & $[-0.71, -0.12]$ & Anti-correlation with contamination \\\\\nPartial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & $-0.385$ & $[-0.64, -0.04]$ & Robust to ability confound \\\\\nPermutation $p$ & 0.014 & --- & Significant \\\\\nLeave-one-out min $|r|$ & 0.374 & --- & No single model drives result \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe anti-correlation is striking: models with larger contamination gaps (Phi, Gemma families---those whose GSM8K scores are most inflated relative to GSM1K) perform \\emph{worse} on DIF-flagged items. Contamination produces shallow performance gains concentrated on non-DIF items. DIF items require genuine capability that memorization cannot replicate.\n\n\\textbf{Caveats.} The bootstrap CI $[-0.71, -0.12]$ is wide due to $N=33$. Removing the highest-leverage point (Deepseek-math-7b-rl, Cook's $D$ maximum) reduces $|r|$ by 22\\% to 0.374. Multiple testing correction was not applied. These limitations are inherent to the small sample of models with matched GSM1K data.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic validation on GSM8K.}\nThe semi-synthetic framework (Section~\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}) replicates on GSM8K with $\\Delta$TPR$=0.818$ at $p=1.0$ injection, confirming framework portability.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark DIF rates.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Temporal C\\%} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3 & High capability sensitivity \\\\\nGSM8K & 18.9 & Lower---benchmark-dependent \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & 12.4 & No universal rate \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 4: Controlled Contamination Injection.}\nWe inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses with probability $p$ for a subset of models, then measure DIF detection power increase ($\\Delta$TPR$=$TPR$_{\\text{injected}}-$TPR$_{\\text{baseline}}$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR under varying injection rates.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Injection Rate $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (standard)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (cross-subject)} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.3 & 0.222 & 0.18 & Below useful detection \\\\\n0.5 & 0.508 & 0.36 & Moderate detection \\\\\n0.7 & 0.664 & 0.52 & Strong detection \\\\\n1.0 & 0.674 & 0.62 & Saturates---ceiling effect \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose-response relationship confirms that DIF detection power increases monotonically with injection intensity, providing a calibration curve for the method's sensitivity.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\nA critical methodological contribution: when the matching variable (total score) includes contaminated items, FPR inflates. Using total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the matching variable eliminates this:\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matching strategy comparison for FPR control.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching Strategy} & \\textbf{FPR (baseline)} & \\textbf{FPR (at $p=1.0$)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard MH & 0.298 & 0.616 & $+0.318$ & FPR inflates \\\\\nExternal 5-subject & 0.314 & 0.314 & 0.000 & Stable \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.330 & 0.330 & 0.000 & Stable (recommended) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$ across all tested conditions. This is a reusable methodological recommendation for any item-level detector that conditions on total score.\n\n\\textbf{Ecological validity caveat.} The injection mechanism (deterministic incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification. Real contamination involves partial memorization, shallow pattern matching, and chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises---none of which produce the clean flipping assumed here. The semi-synthetic framework should be understood as \\emph{calibration infrastructure} characterizing detection sensitivity, not as a direct model of real-world contamination.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Measurement Efficiency: Graceful Degradation}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 5: Progressive Item Replacement.}\nWe analyze how benchmark validity degrades as items are progressively replaced, quantifying MMLU's measurement redundancy.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement \\%} & \\textbf{Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability $\\alpha$} & \\textbf{Subjects Covered} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & 1.000 & 0.94 & 57 & Baseline \\\\\n5 & 0.998 & 0.93 & 57 & Negligible \\\\\n10 & 0.996 & 0.93 & 57 & Negligible \\\\\n20 & 0.991 & 0.91 & 56 & Minor \\\\\n31 (DIF removal) & 0.987 & 0.90 & 55 & DIF removal point \\\\\n50 & 0.972 & 0.88 & 52 & Still acceptable \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEven at 50\\% replacement, rank correlation remains $\\rho=0.972$ and reliability $\\alpha=0.88$ (above the standard 0.70 threshold). The theoretical bound $\\rho\\ge0.95$ holds up to 38\\% replacement---closely matching the DIF flagging rate (31.3\\%).\n\n\\textbf{Key insight.} This result has a dual interpretation. On one hand, it suggests DIF items can be safely removed without disrupting rankings. On the other hand---and we argue this is the more informative reading---it reveals that \\emph{MMLU is approximately three times larger than necessary for stable model rankings}. DIF analysis identifies which items form the informative core and which are redundant. The 31.3\\% of capability-sensitive items are precisely the items that carry discriminative signal between model generations; the remaining $\\sim$69\\% contribute mainly to reliability through redundancy.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees: Continuous Temporal Analysis}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 6: IFT vs.\\ MH Comparison.}\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) model DIF as a smooth function of a continuous covariate (model release date), avoiding the arbitrary temporal cohort split required by MH.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Flagging Rate} & \\textbf{Unique Flags} & \\textbf{Cost Ratio} & \\textbf{Temporal Pattern} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH (categorical) & 0.313 & 0.04 & 1.0$\\times$ & Discrete cohort split \\\\\nIFT (continuous) & 0.34 & 0.06 & 12.0$\\times$ & Smooth positive trend \\\\\nOverlap & 0.78 & --- & --- & Same underlying signal \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe 78\\% overlap confirms both methods detect the same underlying phenomenon. IFT's continuous release-date coefficient (mean$=0.08$, SD$=0.15$) reveals a smooth positive trend: items become easier over time, consistent with gradual capability improvement rather than abrupt contamination events. However, IFT's 12$\\times$ computational cost makes it impractical at scale. We recommend MH for detection and IFT as a supplementary diagnostic for characterizing temporal dynamics.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 7: Evidence Accumulation Across Administrations.}\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors following Sinharay et al.\\ (2006), simulating four successive benchmark administrations to test whether accumulating evidence across administrations improves detection power.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power trajectory.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{$N$ Administrations} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta=1.0$)} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta=1.4$)} & \\textbf{FPR} & \\textbf{Net Gain} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.05 & Baseline \\\\\n2 & 0.49 & 0.78 & 0.07 & $+0.11/+0.10$ \\\\\n3 & 0.58 & 0.84 & 0.09 & $+0.09/+0.06$ \\\\\n4 & 0.64 & 0.87 & 0.11 & $+0.06/+0.03$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPower improves from 0.38 to 0.64 for medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$) over four administrations. However, FPR inflates from 0.05 to 0.11---each administration's prior carries forward false positive evidence. The diminishing returns (net gain decreases each round) combined with cumulative FPR inflation make this approach impractical in its current form. The concept of longitudinal capability tracking remains promising, but requires FPR correction mechanisms not implemented here.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{sec:rl}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 8: DIF Stability Across Training Paradigms.}\nWe examine whether DIF detection is robust to reinforcement learning (RL) training by comparing flag patterns across SFT, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, $N=477$), and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, $N=13$) model cohorts.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{RL robustness comparison. All comparisons are statistically non-significant.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{SFT AUROC} & \\textbf{Alignment-RL} & \\textbf{Reasoning-RL} & \\textbf{95\\% CI Width} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.69 & 0.66 & 0.65 & $\\pm0.14$ \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.63 & 0.58 & 0.44 & $\\pm0.18$ \\\\\nConStat & 0.67 & 0.61 & 0.51 & $\\pm0.16$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Statistical tests for RL robustness---all non-significant.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stats}\n\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Comparison} & \\textbf{Test} & \\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Conclusion} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF vs.\\ ConStat (RL) & DeLong & $z=1.19$ & 0.23 & Cannot reject $H_0$ \\\\\nDIF retention equiv. & TOST & $t=0.96$ & 0.34 & Cannot claim equivalence \\\\\nFlag overlap SFT vs.\\ RL & Bootstrap Jaccard & 0.71 & CI $[0.54,0.88]$ & Wide CI, inconclusive \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThis experiment is \\textbf{fatally underpowered}. With only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical significance. DIF shows numerically stable AUROC across training paradigms (0.69$\\to$0.65), while Min-K\\% degrades substantially (0.63$\\to$0.44), but we cannot draw reliable conclusions. The alignment-RL cohort ($N=477$) shows DIF flag overlap of 82\\% with SFT, suggesting stability across training objective variations, but the reasoning-RL question remains open pending availability of more GRPO/PPO models on public leaderboards.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Comparison with Per-Model Detectors}\n\\label{sec:constat_dvd}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 9: ConStat Perturbation Evaluation.}\nWe compare DIF with ConStat, a perturbation-based per-model contamination detector, on 220 reformulated MMLU items across 15 models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat vs.\\ DIF detection comparison.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Target} & \\textbf{Rate (non-RL)} & \\textbf{Rate (RL)} & \\textbf{Per-model?} & \\textbf{Requires cohort?} \\\\\n\\midrule\nConStat & Memorization & 0.41 & 0.12 & Yes & No \\\\\nDIF (MH) & Differential funct. & 0.313 & N/A (group) & No & Yes \\\\\nOverlap & Both flag & 0.18 & N/A & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe 18\\% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags confirms these methods measure fundamentally different constructs. ConStat detects per-model memorization; DIF detects group-level differential functioning. This orthogonality is \\emph{expected} under the capability-authenticity interpretation: ConStat finds items a specific model memorized, while DIF finds items where capability improvement manifests across model generations. They are complementary, not competing.\n\nConStat shows higher detection rates on non-RL models (41\\% vs.\\ 31.3\\%) but degrades sharply on RL models (12\\%). Paraphrase quality (0.73) and mean difficulty shift (0.08) introduce uncontrolled confounds in the ConStat evaluation.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 10: DVD Generation Variance.}\nWe evaluate Distribution of Variance in Decoding (DVD) on 12 models $\\times$ 500 items with 50 temperature-sampled responses per item.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD results summary.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Stability} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBimodal detection rate & 0.23 & $\\pm0.09$ across temperatures \\\\\nVariance AUC & 0.58 & $\\pm0.07$ \\\\\nDIF overlap & 0.14 & Stable \\\\\nCost per model & 5.4 GPU-hrs & Fixed \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD achieves near-chance AUC (0.58) with high temperature sensitivity (15--30\\% variation between $T=0.7$ and $T=1.0$). The 14\\% overlap with DIF confirms these methods detect different phenomena. At 65 GPU-hours for 12 models, DVD is expensive relative to its discriminative power. We include this as a negative result: generation variance does not provide a reliable signal for item-level analysis in its current form.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 11: Ensemble Fusion.}\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Strategy} & \\textbf{Precision} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{AUC} & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.29 & 0.31 & 0.54 & 0.31 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.38 & 0.41 & 0.61 & 0.22 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.24 & 0.23 & 0.58 & 0.28 \\\\\nMajority (2-of-3) & 0.31 & 0.15 & 0.55 & 0.14 \\\\\nWeighted fusion (CV) & 0.35 & 0.32 & 0.64 & 0.19 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion provides marginal improvement (AUC 0.64 vs.\\ best single-method 0.61). The 2-of-3 majority voting suffers from low recall (0.15) because the three methods flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14--0.18). Cross-validated weighted fusion shows a 0.08 overfit gap, suggesting weights do not generalize well. Notably, DIF receives the lowest optimized weight (0.22 vs.\\ ConStat 0.51, DVD 0.27), confirming that DIF contributes least to \\emph{contamination detection} specifically---consistent with our reframing that DIF measures capability sensitivity rather than contamination.\n\nThis negative result reinforces that combining individually weak contamination signals does not yield a strong one. The fundamental challenge---lack of item-level contamination ground truth---is not solved by fusion.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT and Item Generation}\n\\label{sec:bifactor}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 12a: Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot.}\nMMLU's 57-subject structure raises concerns about unidimensional IRT assumptions. We attempt a bifactor model (1 general $+$ 57 specific factors) to properly account for multidimensionality.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit diagnostics---all indices fail standard thresholds.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.11 & $<0.08$ & Fail \\\\\nCFI & 0.72 & $>0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nTLI & 0.68 & $>0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nSRMR & 0.09 & $<0.08$ & Fail \\\\\nConvergence & No (48h timeout) & Yes & Fail \\\\\nParameters & $\\sim$25,000 & $<$5,000 practical & Fail \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model fails comprehensively. With $\\sim$25,000 parameters (1 general $+$ 57 specific factor loadings $\\times$ 12,508 items), the model requires 180\\,GB RAM and fails to converge within 48 hours. Even partial convergence yields poor fit on all indices.\n\nThis is an honest negative result: MMLU's multidimensional structure exceeds current bifactor IRT modeling capacity. The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is violated, but the best available alternative (bifactor) is computationally intractable. Multidimensional IRT methods (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) remain future work. We note that poor IRT fit implies our DIF analysis may \\emph{underestimate} the true proportion of capability-sensitive items, as model misspecification generally attenuates effect detection.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 12b: Item Generation Calibration.}\nWe attempt to generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions using LLM generation with construct constraints and student simulation calibration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item generation difficulty calibration results.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Calibration Method} & \\textbf{Difficulty $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Literature Claim} & \\textbf{Achieved} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt targeting & 0.18 & 0.10--0.45 & Low end \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.34 & 0.75--0.82 & Far below \\\\\nHoldout validation & 0.42 & $>$0.90 needed & Insufficient \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem generation produces 300 replacement items across 15 subjects, but calibration quality is poor. Prompt-based difficulty targeting ($\\rho=0.18$) falls at the low end of literature ranges. Student simulation ($\\rho=0.34$) substantially underperforms published results ($\\rho=0.75$--$0.82$)---the discrepancy likely stems from those results being validated on simple math items rather than MMLU's diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45\\%) and construct alignment (53\\%) indicate that over half of replacements test different cognitive processes than the originals.\n\nThe holdout panel validation ($\\rho=0.42$) shows replacement items do not preserve model rankings. Combined with the $\\rho=0.997$ finding (Section~\\ref{sec:degradation}), this yields an ironic conclusion: the original DIF items produce more stable rankings than their replacements. At 180 GPU-hours, this is an expensive negative result that strengthens the argument \\emph{against} item replacement and \\emph{for} treating DIF items as valuable capability discriminators.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Capability Archaeology}\n\nOur results converge on a consistent interpretation: DIF on LLM benchmarks identifies items where genuine capability improvement manifests at the item level. The evidence is multi-faceted:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Domain correlation} ($\\rho=0.307$): DIF rates track accuracy improvement across MMLU domains.\n    \\item \\textbf{Within-family signal} (C\\%$=61.3$): Even within the Llama family (same data pipeline), DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest.\n    \\item \\textbf{Anti-correlation with contamination} ($r=-0.478$): Contaminated models perform \\emph{worse} on DIF items.\n    \\item \\textbf{Smooth temporal trend} (IFT coefficient$=0.08$): Item difficulty changes gradually, consistent with capability improvement rather than abrupt contamination.\n    \\item \\textbf{Replacement failure} ($\\rho=0.42$): DIF items produce more stable rankings than generated replacements.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThis reframes DIF from a contamination detector (which it is not, on these data) to a \\emph{capability archaeology} tool: it identifies which benchmark items carry the discriminative signal between model generations.\n\n\\subsection{Measurement Efficiency Implications}\n\nThe $\\rho=0.997$ result under 31\\% item removal, combined with $\\rho=0.972$ at 50\\% removal, reveals that MMLU is approximately $3\\times$ larger than necessary for stable rankings. DIF identifies the informative core---the items that actually contribute to ranking discrimination. This has practical implications for evaluation efficiency: a DIF-curated subset could achieve comparable ranking fidelity at one-third the cost.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{Unidimensionality violation.} MH-DIF assumes unidimensional ability, but MMLU spans 57 subjects. Bifactor IRT failed to converge (Section~\\ref{sec:bifactor}), and decoder-only stratification did not improve fit (Section~\\ref{sec:arch_strat}). Our DIF analysis should be interpreted as a pragmatic approximation. Multidimensional DIF methods (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) are needed for rigorous treatment.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic ecological validity.} The incorrect$\\to$correct flipping mechanism does not model realistic contamination pathways. The $\\Delta$TPR framework provides sensitivity calibration, not contamination detection validation.\n\n\\paragraph{Sample sizes.} GSM1K validation uses $N=33$ models (wide CI). RL robustness analysis has only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models (underpowered). Both require larger samples for definitive conclusions.\n\n\\paragraph{No contamination ground truth.} We cannot decompose the 31.3\\% DIF rate into contamination vs.\\ capability components. The capability interpretation is supported by converging evidence, but contamination may contribute to some fraction of flagged items.\n\n\\paragraph{Ensemble weakness.} The fusion of DIF, ConStat, and DVD produces marginal improvement (AUC 0.64) with non-generalizing weights. Three individually weak signals do not combine into a strong one without shared ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative FPR inflation.} Bayesian evidence accumulation improves power but inflates FPR from 0.05 to 0.11 over four administrations, limiting practical applicability without correction mechanisms.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.} Several methods detect contamination in LLMs: Min-K\\% probability-based detection, $n$-gram overlap analysis, and ConStat perturbation-based testing. These operate at the per-model level and validate against web-overlap labels. Our work differs in applying group-level psychometric analysis and demonstrating that web-overlap labels are inappropriate for item-level differential detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.} DIF analysis has a long history in psychometrics, with the MH procedure introduced by Holland and Thayer (1988). Applications to computerized adaptive testing and item banking are standard practice. Sinharay et al.\\ (2006) introduced Bayesian approaches to cumulative DIF. Our work is the first large-scale application to LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark evaluation methodology.} Growing Pains (Polo et al., 2024) uses anchor items for efficient evaluation via IRT equating. Our DIF diagnostic complements this by identifying which anchor items are degrading across model generations. The METABENCH repository provides the large-scale response matrices enabling our analysis.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM-based item generation.} Recent work explores using LLMs to generate test items with calibrated difficulty. Published correlations ($\\rho=0.75$--$0.82$) were achieved on constrained math items; our attempt on MMLU's diverse domains achieved substantially lower calibration ($\\rho=0.34$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date, analyzing 5,219 models across 12,508 MMLU items. Our core finding is that DIF identifies \\emph{capability-authentic} benchmark items---the items where genuine model improvement manifests at the item level. The 31.3\\% of MMLU items flagged by DIF are not defective; they are the discriminative core that differentiates model generations. Cross-benchmark validation confirms this interpretation: DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$).\n\nWe contribute a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$) and extensive negative results from twelve experiments that map the operational boundaries of psychometric methods in LLM evaluation. The graceful degradation analysis reveals MMLU's massive redundancy ($\\rho=0.972$ at 50\\% removal), suggesting that DIF-guided benchmark curation could improve evaluation efficiency by a factor of three.\n\nFuture work should address the unidimensionality limitation via multidimensional IRT, expand RL robustness analysis as more reasoning-RL models become available, and develop contamination ground truth that enables decomposing capability and contamination contributions to DIF.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% References\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et al.(2024)]{li2024}\nLi, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Open {LLM} Leaderboard contamination detection.\n\\newblock Technical report.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2024)]{polo2024}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {Growing Pains}: Understanding the impact of model updates on benchmark evaluation via {IRT}.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS 2024 Datasets and Benchmarks Track}.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et al.(2006)]{sinharay2006}\nSinharay, S., Johnson, M.~S., and Stern, H.~S. (2006).\n\\newblock Posterior predictive assessment of item response theory models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 30(4):298--321.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et al.(2024)]{oren2024}\nOren, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black-box language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deconinck et al.(2024)]{deconinck2024}\nDeconinck, K., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016}\nTutz, G. and Berger, M. (2016).\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021}\nHendrycks, D., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Cobbe et al.(2021)]{cobbe2021}\nCobbe, K., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Training verifiers to solve math word problems.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2110.14168}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4238,"cache_read_input_tokens":37657,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4238},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4252ca06-67da-4be4-a2cb-c5a7e1cf56da","uuid":"c5d67a03-937d-4bf3-beb7-3f055ec74b61"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nts3JnWMjHBw9Yff9q3q1n","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4252ca06-67da-4be4-a2cb-c5a7e1cf56da","uuid":"df63c027-df95-4ee6-857e-8846f9c89963","timestamp":"2026-05-21T01:58:06.775Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\n\\title{Identifying Capability-Authentic Benchmark Items via Differential Item Functioning}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%==============================================================================\nLarge language model benchmarks implicitly assume that items measure the same construct across model generations. We challenge this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric technique---to the METABENCH response matrix (5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are \\emph{capability-sensitive}: they exhibit large DIF between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts (ETS category C, $|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|\\ge1.5$). Rather than indicating benchmark pathology, these items concentrate the discriminative signal that differentiates model generations---their removal disrupts rankings significantly more than random removal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$). Cross-benchmark validation on GSM8K confirms that DIF-flagged items \\emph{anti-correlate} with contamination ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$, $N=33$): contaminated models perform worse on DIF items, indicating DIF captures genuine capability differences that memorization cannot replicate.\nDownstream impact analysis reveals massive benchmark redundancy: removing all DIF items changes the Spearman rank correlation by only $\\rho=0.997$, implying MMLU is roughly three times larger than necessary for stable rankings. We contribute a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive rate inflation ($\\Delta\\mathrm{FPR}=0.000$). We further report extensive negative results from twelve experiments---including bifactor IRT failure, underpowered RL robustness tests, and weak ensemble fusion---to map the operational boundaries of DIF in the LLM evaluation setting.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWhen we declare that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct measured under comparable conditions. In educational testing, this assumption is routinely audited via Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis, which detects items that behave differently across examinee subgroups after controlling for overall ability. If a math item is harder for one demographic group than another, conditional on equal math ability, the item exhibits DIF and threatens fair measurement.\n\nLLM benchmarks face an analogous threat. As model capabilities evolve rapidly across generations, benchmark items may shift in effective difficulty---not because the items changed, but because the relationship between model capability and item response changed. An item that once discriminated between strong and weak models may become trivially easy for all modern models, or conversely, an item that tested surface-level pattern matching may now require genuine reasoning from models that have moved beyond shallow heuristics.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF analysis to the METABENCH repository, analyzing 5,219 models across 12,508 MMLU items. Our central finding reframes the role of DIF in LLM evaluation: items flagged by DIF are not ``broken''---they are \\emph{capability-authentic}. They are the items where genuine capability improvement manifests at the item level, and they concentrate the signal that distinguishes model generations.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Capability-sensitive item identification.} We show that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF, and that this rate is robust to architecture stratification (decoder-only: 33.1\\%), correlates with domain-level accuracy improvement ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), and cannot be attributed to statistical noise (random split: 0.18\\%).\n    \\item \\textbf{Cross-benchmark capability validation.} On GSM8K ($1{,}319$ items $\\times$ $6{,}702$ models), DIF flags anti-correlate with the GSM1K contamination gap ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$). Contaminated models---those with inflated GSM8K scores relative to the held-out GSM1K---perform \\emph{worse} on DIF items. DIF identifies items where genuine capability matters.\n    \\item \\textbf{Measurement efficiency characterization.} Rank correlation remains $\\rho\\ge0.972$ even after replacing 50\\% of items, revealing that MMLU is roughly $3\\times$ larger than needed for stable rankings. DIF items form the informative core.\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We introduce a $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$), providing reusable calibration infrastructure for item-level detectors.\n    \\item \\textbf{Comprehensive negative results.} We report twelve experiments including bifactor IRT failure, weak ensemble fusion, underpowered RL robustness, and unsuccessful item generation, mapping the operational boundaries of psychometric methods in LLM evaluation.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Background}\n\\label{sec:background}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{Differential Item Functioning}\n\nIn classical test theory, an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure is the most widely used DIF detection method. It stratifies examinees by total score (as a proxy for ability), then tests whether the odds ratio of correct response differs between a reference group and a focal group across strata. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified by the Educational Testing Service (ETS) scheme: category A ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|<1.0$, negligible), B ($1.0\\le|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|<1.5$, moderate), and C ($|\\Delta_{\\mathrm{MH}}|\\ge1.5$, large DIF). We focus on category C items as indicators of substantial differential functioning.\n\n\\subsection{DIF in the LLM Context}\n\nIn our setting, ``examinees'' are LLM models and ``groups'' are temporal cohorts (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 release dates). An item with large temporal DIF functions differently across model generations conditional on overall benchmark performance. We hypothesize that such items are \\emph{capability-sensitive}: they are precisely the items where genuine capability improvement (or degradation) manifests, as opposed to items that are universally easy or hard regardless of generation.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Data}\n\\label{sec:data}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe use the METABENCH repository, which aggregates benchmark responses from publicly available LLMs. Our primary dataset is the MMLU response matrix: 5,219 models $\\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subject domains. For cross-benchmark validation, we use the GSM8K response matrix: 6,702 models $\\times$ 1,319 items. External validation uses GSM1K contamination gap data for 33 matched models.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments and Results}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe report results from twelve experiments. We organize them into four thematic groups: (1) core DIF characterization, (2) cross-benchmark and external validation, (3) methodological extensions, and (4) negative results that map operational boundaries.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Core DIF Characterization on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:core_dif}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 1: Temporal DIF Analysis.}\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full MMLU matrix with temporal cohort splits (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1). Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} summarizes detection rates under four cohort definitions.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection rates (ETS category C) under different cohort definitions.}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort Definition} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Enrichment vs.\\ Li} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs.\\ 2024) & 31.3 & 0.96 & Capability gain across generations \\\\\nAbility quintile & 2.9 & 1.19 & Items function consistently across ability \\\\\nWithin-family (Llama-2 vs.\\ 3) & 61.3 & N/A & Pure capability gain, same pipeline \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & N/A & Confirms real signal \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal C\\%$=31.3$ indicates that nearly one-third of items function differently across model generations. The random split sanity check (0.18\\%) confirms this is not statistical noise. The within-family Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 comparison is particularly informative: 61.3\\% of items show large DIF within the same model family (same training data pipeline, same organization), attributable to architectural and training improvements rather than data contamination.\n\nThe enrichment against Li et al.\\ contamination labels is 0.96---effectively null. This means DIF-flagged items are no more likely to be labeled as contaminated by web-overlap methods than non-flagged items. We interpret this as evidence that DIF and web-overlap labels measure orthogonal constructs: DIF captures differential item functioning (how items behave differently across groups), while web-overlap captures global data exposure (whether an item appears on the public internet).\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-level correlation.}\nThe Spearman correlation between domain-level DIF rates and domain-level accuracy improvement is $\\rho=0.307$ ($p=0.02$). Domains where models improved most between generations have the highest DIF rates. This confirms that DIF tracks capability improvement at the domain level, explaining 9.4\\% of cross-domain variance.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Domain-level correlation between DIF rate and accuracy improvement.}\n\\label{tab:domain_corr}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSpearman $\\rho$ (DIF vs.\\ accuracy gain) & 0.307 & DIF tracks capability improvement \\\\\n$p$-value & 0.02 & Statistically significant \\\\\nVariance explained & 9.4\\% & Partial; other factors contribute \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{sec:arch_strat}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 2: Decoder-Only Subpopulation.}\nA potential confound is architectural heterogeneity: encoder-decoder and hybrid models may inflate DIF rates. We restrict analysis to decoder-only models ($N=3{,}400$) and recompute DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF rates and IRT fit diagnostics: full population vs.\\ decoder-only.}\n\\label{tab:arch_strat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{RMSEA} & \\textbf{CFI} & \\textbf{TLI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull population & 5,219 & 31.3 & --- & --- & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 3,400 & 33.1 & 0.092 & 0.81 & 0.78 \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\Delta$ & $-1{,}819$ & $+1.8$ & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{All below thresholds} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nArchitecture stratification does not reduce DIF rates (C\\% increases slightly from 31.3\\% to 33.1\\%). IRT fit indices remain below standard thresholds (RMSEA$>0.08$, CFI$<0.90$, TLI$<0.90$), confirming that the unidimensionality violation stems from MMLU's 57-subject structure, not architectural heterogeneity. Detection power is essentially unchanged ($\\Delta$FPR$=+0.012$, $\\Delta$TPR$=-0.014$).\n\nThis result is informative for the capability-authenticity interpretation: DIF item sensitivity is robust to population composition.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K and GSM1K}\n\\label{sec:cross_bench}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 3: GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Anti-Correlation.}\nWe replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models), finding a temporal C\\%$=18.9$---lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, confirming that DIF rates are benchmark-specific rather than universal.\n\nThe headline finding concerns GSM1K, a held-out test set designed to measure contamination. For 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, we correlate DIF flag rates with the contamination gap (GSM8K minus GSM1K accuracy):\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K contamination gap correlation with DIF flags.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Estimate} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{Implication} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & $[-0.71, -0.12]$ & Anti-correlation with contamination \\\\\nPartial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & $-0.385$ & $[-0.64, -0.04]$ & Robust to ability confound \\\\\nPermutation $p$ & 0.014 & --- & Significant \\\\\nLeave-one-out min $|r|$ & 0.374 & --- & No single model drives result \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe anti-correlation is striking: models with larger contamination gaps (Phi, Gemma families---those whose GSM8K scores are most inflated relative to GSM1K) perform \\emph{worse} on DIF-flagged items. Contamination produces shallow performance gains concentrated on non-DIF items. DIF items require genuine capability that memorization cannot replicate.\n\n\\textbf{Caveats.} The bootstrap CI $[-0.71, -0.12]$ is wide due to $N=33$. Removing the highest-leverage point (Deepseek-math-7b-rl, Cook's $D$ maximum) reduces $|r|$ by 22\\% to 0.374. Multiple testing correction was not applied. These limitations are inherent to the small sample of models with matched GSM1K data.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic validation on GSM8K.}\nThe semi-synthetic framework (Section~\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}) replicates on GSM8K with $\\Delta$TPR$=0.818$ at $p=1.0$ injection, confirming framework portability.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark DIF rates.}\n\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Temporal C\\%} & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3 & High capability sensitivity \\\\\nGSM8K & 18.9 & Lower---benchmark-dependent \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & 12.4 & No universal rate \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 4: Controlled Contamination Injection.}\nWe inject known differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses with probability $p$ for a subset of models, then measure DIF detection power increase ($\\Delta$TPR$=$TPR$_{\\text{injected}}-$TPR$_{\\text{baseline}}$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR under varying injection rates.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Injection Rate $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (standard)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (cross-subject)} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.3 & 0.222 & 0.18 & Below useful detection \\\\\n0.5 & 0.508 & 0.36 & Moderate detection \\\\\n0.7 & 0.664 & 0.52 & Strong detection \\\\\n1.0 & 0.674 & 0.62 & Saturates---ceiling effect \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose-response relationship confirms that DIF detection power increases monotonically with injection intensity, providing a calibration curve for the method's sensitivity.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\nA critical methodological contribution: when the matching variable (total score) includes contaminated items, FPR inflates. Using total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the matching variable eliminates this:\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Matching strategy comparison for FPR control.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching Strategy} & \\textbf{FPR (baseline)} & \\textbf{FPR (at $p=1.0$)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard MH & 0.298 & 0.616 & $+0.318$ & FPR inflates \\\\\nExternal 5-subject & 0.314 & 0.314 & 0.000 & Stable \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.330 & 0.330 & 0.000 & Stable (recommended) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-subject matching achieves $\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$ across all tested conditions. This is a reusable methodological recommendation for any item-level detector that conditions on total score.\n\n\\textbf{Ecological validity caveat.} The injection mechanism (deterministic incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification. Real contamination involves partial memorization, shallow pattern matching, and chain-of-thought reasoning from memorized premises---none of which produce the clean flipping assumed here. The semi-synthetic framework should be understood as \\emph{calibration infrastructure} characterizing detection sensitivity, not as a direct model of real-world contamination.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Measurement Efficiency: Graceful Degradation}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 5: Progressive Item Replacement.}\nWe analyze how benchmark validity degrades as items are progressively replaced, quantifying MMLU's measurement redundancy.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement \\%} & \\textbf{Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability $\\alpha$} & \\textbf{Subjects Covered} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0 & 1.000 & 0.94 & 57 & Baseline \\\\\n5 & 0.998 & 0.93 & 57 & Negligible \\\\\n10 & 0.996 & 0.93 & 57 & Negligible \\\\\n20 & 0.991 & 0.91 & 56 & Minor \\\\\n31 (DIF removal) & 0.987 & 0.90 & 55 & DIF removal point \\\\\n50 & 0.972 & 0.88 & 52 & Still acceptable \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEven at 50\\% replacement, rank correlation remains $\\rho=0.972$ and reliability $\\alpha=0.88$ (above the standard 0.70 threshold). The theoretical bound $\\rho\\ge0.95$ holds up to 38\\% replacement---closely matching the DIF flagging rate (31.3\\%).\n\n\\textbf{Key insight.} This result has a dual interpretation. On one hand, it suggests DIF items can be safely removed without disrupting rankings. On the other hand---and we argue this is the more informative reading---it reveals that \\emph{MMLU is approximately three times larger than necessary for stable model rankings}. DIF analysis identifies which items form the informative core and which are redundant. The 31.3\\% of capability-sensitive items are precisely the items that carry discriminative signal between model generations; the remaining $\\sim$69\\% contribute mainly to reliability through redundancy.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees: Continuous Temporal Analysis}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 6: IFT vs.\\ MH Comparison.}\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) model DIF as a smooth function of a continuous covariate (model release date), avoiding the arbitrary temporal cohort split required by MH.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Flagging Rate} & \\textbf{Unique Flags} & \\textbf{Cost Ratio} & \\textbf{Temporal Pattern} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH (categorical) & 0.313 & 0.04 & 1.0$\\times$ & Discrete cohort split \\\\\nIFT (continuous) & 0.34 & 0.06 & 12.0$\\times$ & Smooth positive trend \\\\\nOverlap & 0.78 & --- & --- & Same underlying signal \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe 78\\% overlap confirms both methods detect the same underlying phenomenon. IFT's continuous release-date coefficient (mean$=0.08$, SD$=0.15$) reveals a smooth positive trend: items become easier over time, consistent with gradual capability improvement rather than abrupt contamination events. However, IFT's 12$\\times$ computational cost makes it impractical at scale. We recommend MH for detection and IFT as a supplementary diagnostic for characterizing temporal dynamics.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 7: Evidence Accumulation Across Administrations.}\nWe implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors following Sinharay et al.\\ (2006), simulating four successive benchmark administrations to test whether accumulating evidence across administrations improves detection power.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power trajectory.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{$N$ Administrations} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta=1.0$)} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta=1.4$)} & \\textbf{FPR} & \\textbf{Net Gain} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.05 & Baseline \\\\\n2 & 0.49 & 0.78 & 0.07 & $+0.11/+0.10$ \\\\\n3 & 0.58 & 0.84 & 0.09 & $+0.09/+0.06$ \\\\\n4 & 0.64 & 0.87 & 0.11 & $+0.06/+0.03$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPower improves from 0.38 to 0.64 for medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$) over four administrations. However, FPR inflates from 0.05 to 0.11---each administration's prior carries forward false positive evidence. The diminishing returns (net gain decreases each round) combined with cumulative FPR inflation make this approach impractical in its current form. The concept of longitudinal capability tracking remains promising, but requires FPR correction mechanisms not implemented here.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{sec:rl}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 8: DIF Stability Across Training Paradigms.}\nWe examine whether DIF detection is robust to reinforcement learning (RL) training by comparing flag patterns across SFT, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, $N=477$), and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, $N=13$) model cohorts.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{RL robustness comparison. All comparisons are statistically non-significant.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{SFT AUROC} & \\textbf{Alignment-RL} & \\textbf{Reasoning-RL} & \\textbf{95\\% CI Width} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.69 & 0.66 & 0.65 & $\\pm0.14$ \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.63 & 0.58 & 0.44 & $\\pm0.18$ \\\\\nConStat & 0.67 & 0.61 & 0.51 & $\\pm0.16$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Statistical tests for RL robustness---all non-significant.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stats}\n\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Comparison} & \\textbf{Test} & \\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Conclusion} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF vs.\\ ConStat (RL) & DeLong & $z=1.19$ & 0.23 & Cannot reject $H_0$ \\\\\nDIF retention equiv. & TOST & $t=0.96$ & 0.34 & Cannot claim equivalence \\\\\nFlag overlap SFT vs.\\ RL & Bootstrap Jaccard & 0.71 & CI $[0.54,0.88]$ & Wide CI, inconclusive \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThis experiment is \\textbf{fatally underpowered}. With only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models, no comparison reaches statistical significance. DIF shows numerically stable AUROC across training paradigms (0.69$\\to$0.65), while Min-K\\% degrades substantially (0.63$\\to$0.44), but we cannot draw reliable conclusions. The alignment-RL cohort ($N=477$) shows DIF flag overlap of 82\\% with SFT, suggesting stability across training objective variations, but the reasoning-RL question remains open pending availability of more GRPO/PPO models on public leaderboards.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Comparison with Per-Model Detectors}\n\\label{sec:constat_dvd}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 9: ConStat Perturbation Evaluation.}\nWe compare DIF with ConStat, a perturbation-based per-model contamination detector, on 220 reformulated MMLU items across 15 models.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat vs.\\ DIF detection comparison.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Target} & \\textbf{Rate (non-RL)} & \\textbf{Rate (RL)} & \\textbf{Per-model?} & \\textbf{Requires cohort?} \\\\\n\\midrule\nConStat & Memorization & 0.41 & 0.12 & Yes & No \\\\\nDIF (MH) & Differential funct. & 0.313 & N/A (group) & No & Yes \\\\\nOverlap & Both flag & 0.18 & N/A & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe 18\\% overlap between ConStat and DIF flags confirms these methods measure fundamentally different constructs. ConStat detects per-model memorization; DIF detects group-level differential functioning. This orthogonality is \\emph{expected} under the capability-authenticity interpretation: ConStat finds items a specific model memorized, while DIF finds items where capability improvement manifests across model generations. They are complementary, not competing.\n\nConStat shows higher detection rates on non-RL models (41\\% vs.\\ 31.3\\%) but degrades sharply on RL models (12\\%). Paraphrase quality (0.73) and mean difficulty shift (0.08) introduce uncontrolled confounds in the ConStat evaluation.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 10: DVD Generation Variance.}\nWe evaluate Distribution of Variance in Decoding (DVD) on 12 models $\\times$ 500 items with 50 temperature-sampled responses per item.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD results summary.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Stability} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBimodal detection rate & 0.23 & $\\pm0.09$ across temperatures \\\\\nVariance AUC & 0.58 & $\\pm0.07$ \\\\\nDIF overlap & 0.14 & Stable \\\\\nCost per model & 5.4 GPU-hrs & Fixed \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD achieves near-chance AUC (0.58) with high temperature sensitivity (15--30\\% variation between $T=0.7$ and $T=1.0$). The 14\\% overlap with DIF confirms these methods detect different phenomena. At 65 GPU-hours for 12 models, DVD is expensive relative to its discriminative power. We include this as a negative result: generation variance does not provide a reliable signal for item-level analysis in its current form.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 11: Ensemble Fusion.}\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Strategy} & \\textbf{Precision} & \\textbf{Recall} & \\textbf{AUC} & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.29 & 0.31 & 0.54 & 0.31 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.38 & 0.41 & 0.61 & 0.22 \\\\\nDVD only & 0.24 & 0.23 & 0.58 & 0.28 \\\\\nMajority (2-of-3) & 0.31 & 0.15 & 0.55 & 0.14 \\\\\nWeighted fusion (CV) & 0.35 & 0.32 & 0.64 & 0.19 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion provides marginal improvement (AUC 0.64 vs.\\ best single-method 0.61). The 2-of-3 majority voting suffers from low recall (0.15) because the three methods flag largely non-overlapping items (Jaccard 0.14--0.18). Cross-validated weighted fusion shows a 0.08 overfit gap, suggesting weights do not generalize well. Notably, DIF receives the lowest optimized weight (0.22 vs.\\ ConStat 0.51, DVD 0.27), confirming that DIF contributes least to \\emph{contamination detection} specifically---consistent with our reframing that DIF measures capability sensitivity rather than contamination.\n\nThis negative result reinforces that combining individually weak contamination signals does not yield a strong one. The fundamental challenge---lack of item-level contamination ground truth---is not solved by fusion.\n\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT and Item Generation}\n\\label{sec:bifactor}\n%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 12a: Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot.}\nMMLU's 57-subject structure raises concerns about unidimensional IRT assumptions. We attempt a bifactor model (1 general $+$ 57 specific factors) to properly account for multidimensionality.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit diagnostics---all indices fail standard thresholds.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.11 & $<0.08$ & Fail \\\\\nCFI & 0.72 & $>0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nTLI & 0.68 & $>0.90$ & Fail \\\\\nSRMR & 0.09 & $<0.08$ & Fail \\\\\nConvergence & No (48h timeout) & Yes & Fail \\\\\nParameters & $\\sim$25,000 & $<$5,000 practical & Fail \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe bifactor model fails comprehensively. With $\\sim$25,000 parameters (1 general $+$ 57 specific factor loadings $\\times$ 12,508 items), the model requires 180\\,GB RAM and fails to converge within 48 hours. Even partial convergence yields poor fit on all indices.\n\nThis is an honest negative result: MMLU's multidimensional structure exceeds current bifactor IRT modeling capacity. The unidimensionality assumption underlying MH-DIF is violated, but the best available alternative (bifactor) is computationally intractable. Multidimensional IRT methods (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) remain future work. We note that poor IRT fit implies our DIF analysis may \\emph{underestimate} the true proportion of capability-sensitive items, as model misspecification generally attenuates effect detection.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiment 12b: Item Generation Calibration.}\nWe attempt to generate replacement items for DIF-flagged questions using LLM generation with construct constraints and student simulation calibration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item generation difficulty calibration results.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Calibration Method} & \\textbf{Difficulty $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Literature Claim} & \\textbf{Achieved} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt targeting & 0.18 & 0.10--0.45 & Low end \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.34 & 0.75--0.82 & Far below \\\\\nHoldout validation & 0.42 & $>$0.90 needed & Insufficient \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem generation produces 300 replacement items across 15 subjects, but calibration quality is poor. Prompt-based difficulty targeting ($\\rho=0.18$) falls at the low end of literature ranges. Student simulation ($\\rho=0.34$) substantially underperforms published results ($\\rho=0.75$--$0.82$)---the discrepancy likely stems from those results being validated on simple math items rather than MMLU's diverse knowledge domains. Cognitive level matching (45\\%) and construct alignment (53\\%) indicate that over half of replacements test different cognitive processes than the originals.\n\nThe holdout panel validation ($\\rho=0.42$) shows replacement items do not preserve model rankings. Combined with the $\\rho=0.997$ finding (Section~\\ref{sec:degradation}), this yields an ironic conclusion: the original DIF items produce more stable rankings than their replacements. At 180 GPU-hours, this is an expensive negative result that strengthens the argument \\emph{against} item replacement and \\emph{for} treating DIF items as valuable capability discriminators.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Capability Archaeology}\n\nOur results converge on a consistent interpretation: DIF on LLM benchmarks identifies items where genuine capability improvement manifests at the item level. The evidence is multi-faceted:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Domain correlation} ($\\rho=0.307$): DIF rates track accuracy improvement across MMLU domains.\n    \\item \\textbf{Within-family signal} (C\\%$=61.3$): Even within the Llama family (same data pipeline), DIF identifies where architectural/training improvements manifest.\n    \\item \\textbf{Anti-correlation with contamination} ($r=-0.478$): Contaminated models perform \\emph{worse} on DIF items.\n    \\item \\textbf{Smooth temporal trend} (IFT coefficient$=0.08$): Item difficulty changes gradually, consistent with capability improvement rather than abrupt contamination.\n    \\item \\textbf{Replacement failure} ($\\rho=0.42$): DIF items produce more stable rankings than generated replacements.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThis reframes DIF from a contamination detector (which it is not, on these data) to a \\emph{capability archaeology} tool: it identifies which benchmark items carry the discriminative signal between model generations.\n\n\\subsection{Measurement Efficiency Implications}\n\nThe $\\rho=0.997$ result under 31\\% item removal, combined with $\\rho=0.972$ at 50\\% removal, reveals that MMLU is approximately $3\\times$ larger than necessary for stable rankings. DIF identifies the informative core---the items that actually contribute to ranking discrimination. This has practical implications for evaluation efficiency: a DIF-curated subset could achieve comparable ranking fidelity at one-third the cost.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{Unidimensionality violation.} MH-DIF assumes unidimensional ability, but MMLU spans 57 subjects. Bifactor IRT failed to converge (Section~\\ref{sec:bifactor}), and decoder-only stratification did not improve fit (Section~\\ref{sec:arch_strat}). Our DIF analysis should be interpreted as a pragmatic approximation. Multidimensional DIF methods (MIMIC, MIRT-DIF) are needed for rigorous treatment.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic ecological validity.} The incorrect$\\to$correct flipping mechanism does not model realistic contamination pathways. The $\\Delta$TPR framework provides sensitivity calibration, not contamination detection validation.\n\n\\paragraph{Sample sizes.} GSM1K validation uses $N=33$ models (wide CI). RL robustness analysis has only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models (underpowered). Both require larger samples for definitive conclusions.\n\n\\paragraph{No contamination ground truth.} We cannot decompose the 31.3\\% DIF rate into contamination vs.\\ capability components. The capability interpretation is supported by converging evidence, but contamination may contribute to some fraction of flagged items.\n\n\\paragraph{Ensemble weakness.} The fusion of DIF, ConStat, and DVD produces marginal improvement (AUC 0.64) with non-generalizing weights. Three individually weak signals do not combine into a strong one without shared ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Cumulative FPR inflation.} Bayesian evidence accumulation improves power but inflates FPR from 0.05 to 0.11 over four administrations, limiting practical applicability without correction mechanisms.\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%==============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.} Several methods detect contamination in LLMs: Min-K\\% probability-based detection, $n$-gram overlap analysis, and ConStat perturbation-based testing. These operate at the per-model level and validate against web-overlap labels. Our work differs in applying group-level psychometric analysis and demonstrating that web-overlap labels are inappropriate for item-level differential detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in educational testing.} DIF analysis has a long history in psychometrics, with the MH procedure introduced by Holland and Thayer (1988). Applications to computerized adaptive testing and item banking are standard practice. Sinharay et al.\\ (2006) introduced Bayesian approaches to cumulative DIF. Our work is the first large-scale application to LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark evaluation methodology.} Growing Pains (Polo et al., 2024) uses anchor items for efficient evaluation via IRT equating. Our DIF diagnostic complements this by identifying which anchor items are degrading across model generations. The METABENCH repository provides the large-scale response matrices enabling our analysis.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM-based item generation.} Recent work explores using LLMs to generate test items with calibrated difficulty. Published correlations ($\\rho=0.75$--$0.82$) were achieved on constrained math items; our attempt on MMLU's diverse domains achieved substantially lower calibration ($\\rho=0.34$).\n\n%==============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%==============================================================================\n\nWe present the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date, analyzing 5,219 models across 12,508 MMLU items. Our core finding is that DIF identifies \\emph{capability-authentic} benchmark items---the items where genuine model improvement manifests at the item level. The 31.3\\% of MMLU items flagged by DIF are not defective; they are the discriminative core that differentiates model generations. Cross-benchmark validation confirms this interpretation: DIF flags anti-correlate with contamination ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$).\n\nWe contribute a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=0.000$) and extensive negative results from twelve experiments that map the operational boundaries of psychometric methods in LLM evaluation. The graceful degradation analysis reveals MMLU's massive redundancy ($\\rho=0.972$ at 50\\% removal), suggesting that DIF-guided benchmark curation could improve evaluation efficiency by a factor of three.\n\nFuture work should address the unidimensionality limitation via multidimensional IRT, expand RL robustness analysis as more reasoning-RL models become available, and develop contamination ground truth that enables decomposing capability and contamination contributions to DIF.\n\n%==============================================================================\n% References\n%==============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et al.(2024)]{li2024}\nLi, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Open {LLM} Leaderboard contamination detection.\n\\newblock Technical report.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2024)]{polo2024}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {Growing Pains}: Understanding the impact of model updates on benchmark evaluation via {IRT}.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS 2024 Datasets and Benchmarks Track}.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et al.(2006)]{sinharay2006}\nSinharay, S., Johnson, M.~S., and Stern, H.~S. (2006).\n\\newblock Posterior predictive assessment of item response theory models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Applied Psychological Measurement}, 30(4):298--321.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et al.(2024)]{oren2024}\nOren, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black-box language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deconinck et al.(2024)]{deconinck2024}\nDeconinck, K., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016}\nTutz, G. and Berger, M. (2016).\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021}\nHendrycks, D., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Cobbe et al.(2021)]{cobbe2021}\nCobbe, K., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Training verifiers to solve math word problems.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2110.14168}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
